(window.PHASE_DATA=window.PHASE_DATA||{})[4]={"pages": [{"pdf": 168, "printed": 141, "ch": 0, "text": "PHASE 4\n\nEconometrics: IV, GMM and Dynamic\nPanels\nChapters 19–26\n\nWooldridge (2010), Chapters 5, 8, 11, 14; Blundell and Bond (1998); Roodman (2009)"}, {"pdf": 169, "printed": 142, "ch": 19, "text": "CHAPTER 19\n\nPrerequisites for Phase 4: the Algebra\nand Asymptotics You Need\nWhat this chapter is\nNot a reading summary: a toolkit. Chapters 20–25 use only the ideas collected here. Every result is derived or its derivation is pointed to; the derivations are mine, written from standard econometric theory, and match the treatment in Wooldridge (2010), Chapters 2–4 and 10 (the chapters that precede the ones you were assigned). Skim sections you know; the self-test below tells you which.\n\n19.1 Self-test: can you skip a section?\nIf you can already…\n\nSkip\n\nShow that P = Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ is symmetric and idempotent and say what P y is\n\n19.2\n\nExplain why plim β̂OLS = β + Cov(x, u)/Var(x)\n\n19.5\n\nState the Slutsky theorem and the delta method\n\n19.4\n\nDerive the within estimator and say why it needs strict exogeneity\n\n19.6\n\n19.2 Matrix algebra used throughout\nNotation. A column vector is x; its transpose is x′ . X is n × k (rows are observations). In is the identity. A is symmetric if A = A′ ; it is positive definite (p.d.) if c′ Ac > 0 for every c 6= 0 and positive semidefinite (p.s.d.) if c′ Ac ≥ 0. For symmetric A, B we write A \u0017 B if A − B is p.s.d.; this is what “one estimator is at least as efficient as another” means for variance matrices. Rank and inverses. rank(A) is the number of linearly independent columns. If X (n × k) has rank k then X ′ X is p.d. and invertible. Proof: c′ X ′ Xc = kXck2 ≥ 0, and it is zero only if Xc = 0, which rank k rules out unless c = 0. Projection. For a full-column-rank Z define PZ = Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ and MZ = I − PZ . Result 19.1 (Projection matrices). PZ and MZ are symmetric and idempotent (PZ PZ = PZ ); PZ Z = Z, MZ Z = 0, PZ MZ = 0; rank(PZ ) = tr(PZ ) = rank(Z)."}, {"pdf": 170, "printed": 143, "ch": 19, "text": "Proof. Symmetry: (Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ )′ = Z((Z ′ Z)−1 )′ Z ′ = PZ since Z ′ Z is symmetric. Idempotence: PZ PZ = Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ = Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ = PZ . PZ Z = Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ Z = Z, hence MZ Z = Z − Z = 0 and MZ PZ = MZ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ = 0. Trace: tr(PZ ) = tr (Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ Z = tr(Ik ) = k using tr(AB) = tr(BA); an idempotent matrix has eigenvalues 0 or 1, so its trace is its rank. Meaning: PZ y is the vector of fitted values from regressing y on Z; it is the point of the column space of Z closest to y. Two-stage least squares (Chapter 20) is built on exactly this object. Minimising a quadratic form. For a p.s.d. weighting matrix W , the minimiser of (y − Xb)′ W (y − Xb) solves the first-order condition −2X ′ W (y − Xb) = 0 (derivative of b′ Ab is 2Ab for symmetric A), so b̂ = (X ′ W X)−1 X ′ W y if X ′ W X is invertible. This is the algebra behind both OLS (W = I) and GMM (Chapter 21). The Kronecker product. Wooldridge writes Zi = IG ⊗ zi for “the same instruments in every equation”. A ⊗ B replaces each entry aij of A by the block aij B; for A = IG this is a block-diagonal matrix with B repeated G times. Nothing more is needed here.\n\n19.3 Probability tools\nExpectation, variance, covariance. Var(x) = E[(x − Ex)(x − Ex)′ ], Cov(x, y) = E[(x − Ex)(y − Ey)′ ]. If a is a constant vector, Var(a′ x) = a′ Var(x)a. Conditional expectation and the law of iterated expectations (LIE). E(y|x) is the best predictor of y given x in the mean-squared sense. LIE: E(y) = E[E(y|x)] and, more generally, E(y|x) = E[E(y|x, w)|x]. Corollary used repeatedly: if E(u|x) = 0 then E(xu) = 0 (because E(xu) = E[xE(u|x)] = 0). The converse is false: uncorrelated does not imply meanindependent. Many econometric statements (“exogenous”, “predetermined”) are statements of this kind; their strength differs, and Chapter 23 depends on the difference. Linear projection. The linear projection of y on x is L(y|x) = xβ with β = [E(x′ x)]−1 E(x′ y) (Wooldridge 2010, Section 2.3). It exists whenever E(x′ x) is invertible and is defined by E[x′ (y− xβ)] = 0. It equals the conditional expectation only when the latter is linear.\n\n19.4 Asymptotics\n\nConvergence in probability. θ̂N −\n→ θ (plim θ̂N = θ) means P (|θ̂N − θ| > ε) → 0 for every\nε > 0. An estimator with this property is consistent.\nWeak law of large numbers (WLLN).\nE(wi ).\n\nIf wi are i.i.d. with E|wi | < ∞, then N −1\n\n143\n\ni wi −"}, {"pdf": 171, "printed": 144, "ch": 19, "text": "Central limit theorem (CLT). If wi are i.i.d. with mean µ and finite variance Σ, then → Normal(0, Σ). N −1/2 i (wi − µ) − Slutsky and continuous mapping.\n\nIf ÂN −\n→ A and g is continuous at A, then g(ÂN ) −\n\ng(A). In particular (ÂN )−1 −\n→ A−1 if A is invertible. If ÂN −\n→ A and ξN −\n→ ξ then ÂN ξN −\n→ Aξ.\n\nN (θ̂ − θ) −\n→ Normal(0, V ) and h is continuously differentiable with\nJacobian H = ∂h/∂θ′ , then N (h(θ̂) − h(θ)) −\n→ Normal(0, HV H ′ ). Proof sketch: mean-value\nexpansion h(θ̂) = h(θ) + H(θ̄)(θ̂ − θ) and Slutsky.\nThe delta method.\n\nMean-value expansion. The workhorse of Chapter 22: if f is differentiable, f (θ̂) = f (θ0 ) + f ′ (θ̄)(θ̂ − θ0 ) for some θ̄ between them, and θ̄ − → θ0 whenever θ̂ − → θ0 . Fixed T , large N In every panel-data result of this phase, N → ∞ and T is fixed. A bank panel with 12 banks and 15 years is not large-N by any stretch; Chapter 26 returns to what that means for your thesis.\n\n19.5 OLS: the derivation you will reuse\nModel y = xβ + u with x a 1 × K row. Stack N observations: y = Xβ + u. Minimising the sum of squares gives the normal equations X ′ (y − X β̂) = 0, so \u0011−1 \u0010 β̂ = (X ′ X)−1 X ′ y = β + (X ′ X)−1 X ′ u = β + N −1 x′i xi N −1 x′i ui . Result 19.2 (Consistency and asymptotic normality of OLS). If (i) E(x′ u) = 0 and (ii) E(x′ x) is invertible, with i.i.d. data and finite moments, then β̂ − → β and N (β̂ − β) − Normal 0, A−1 BA−1 with A = E(x′ x), B = E(u2 x′ x). Proof. By the WLLN, N −1 x′i xi − → A and N −1 x′i ui − → E(x′ u) = 0; by Slutsky β̂ − β − A−1 · 0 = 0. For normality write N (β̂ − β) = (N −1 x′i xi )−1 N −1/2 x′i ui . The summands x′i ui have mean 0 and variance B, so the CLT gives N −1/2 x′i ui − → Normal(0, B); apply Slutsky. The matrix A−1 BA−1 is the sandwich (heteroskedasticity-robust) variance; it simplifies to σ 2 A−1 if E(u2 |x) = σ 2 . When (i) fails: endogeneity. For a single regressor, plim β̂ = β +Cov(x, u)/Var(x). This is the whole motivation for instrumental variables: Chapter 20 replaces x in the moment condition by a variable z that is uncorrelated with u."}, {"pdf": 172, "printed": 145, "ch": 19, "text": "Numerical check (mine). Let x = z + q, u = q + e with z, q, e independent standard normals. Then Cov(x, u) = Var(q) = 1, Var(x) = 2, so OLS converges to β + 0.5. In my simulation with N = 106 and β = 2 the OLS slope is 2.50 and the IV slope using z is 2.00, to two decimals.\n\n19.6 Panel notation and the two basic transformations\nObservations are indexed i = 1, . . . , N (units: banks) and t = 1, . . . , T (years). The workhorse model is yit = xit β + ci + uit , (19.1) where ci is an unobserved effect (a bank’s permanent characteristics) that is allowed to be correlated with xit , and uit is the idiosyncratic error. Pooled OLS on (19.1) is inconsistent when Cov(xit , ci ) 6= 0, since ci sits in the error. Within (fixed-effects) transformation. Average (19.1) over t: ȳi = x̄i β +ci + ūi . Subtract: ÿit = ẍit β + üit with ÿit = yit − ȳi . The ci vanish. In matrix form the transformation is Q = IT − T −1 ιι′ (with ι a vector of ones); Q is a projection matrix like MZ , and OLS on the transformed data is \u0011−1 X β̂F E = Xi′ QXi Xi′ Qyi .\n\nFirst differencing. Subtract (19.1) at t − 1 from t: ∆yit = ∆xit β + ∆uit .\nResult 19.3 (When FE and FD are consistent). Suppose strict exogeneity holds:\nE(uit |xi1 , . . . , xiT , ci ) = 0 for all t.\nThen E(ẍ′it üit ) = 0 and FE is consistent (fixed T , N → ∞) provided\n\nt E(ẍit ẍit ) has full rank.\n\nProof. üit = uit − T −1 s uis and ẍit is a function of (xi1 , . . . , xiT ). Strict exogeneity makes every uis mean-independent of every xir , so E(ẍ′it üit ) = 0. Then β̂F E − β = (N −1 Xi′ QXi )−1 N −1 Xi′ Qui − → 0 by the WLLN. Why this matters in Chapter 23: a lagged dependent variable yi,t−1 is not strictly exogenous: yi,t−1 depends on ui,t−1 , and the within transformation puts ui,t−1 into üit through ūi , and uit into ÿi,t−1 through ȳi . The correlation that results is the Nickell bias.\n\n19.7 Check yourself\nRecall\nQ19.1 State the WLLN, the CLT and Slutsky’s theorem in one line each.\nQ19.2 What are the defining properties of PZ and MZ ?"}, {"pdf": 173, "printed": 146, "ch": 19, "text": "Q19.3 Define strict exogeneity of xit in model (19.1).\nExplain\nQ19.4 Why is E(xu) = 0 weaker than E(u|x) = 0? Give a case where the first holds and the second does not.\nQ19.5 Why does fixed T matter for the choice of asymptotic framework?\nQ19.6 Why does the within transformation remove ci but not create a new problem when xit is strictly exogenous? Apply\nQ19.7 Show that tr(PZ ) = rank(Z) for Z with L columns, and say what this implies about the number of overidentifying restrictions in a regression of x on Z.\nQ19.8 Let x = z + q and u = q + e as in the example. Find the plim of OLS and of IV using z as instrument.\nQ19.9 Compute β̂F E for N = 2, T = 2 with data (y11 , y12 ) = (1, 3), (x11 , x12 ) = (0, 2), (y21 , y22 ) = (2, 3), (x21 , x22 ) = (1, 2). Viva\nQ19.10 “Consistency is a large-sample property, so it is irrelevant to a panel of 12 banks.” Discuss.\nQ19.11 “FE and FD give the same estimate when T = 2.” Prove it, and say what changes for T > 2.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA19.1 WLLN: sample means converge in probability to expectations. CLT: standardised sums are asymptotically normal. Slutsky: continuous functions of convergent sequences converge to the function of the limit; products of p- and d-convergent sequences converge in distribution.\nA19.2 Symmetric, idempotent; PZ Z = Z, MZ Z = 0.\nA19.3 E(uit |xi1 , . . . , xiT , ci ) = 0 for all t.\nA19.4 E(u|x) = 0 implies zero correlation with every function of x; the converse fails: let u = x2 − 1 with x standard normal, so E(xu) = E(x3 ) − E(x) = 0 but E(u|x) 6= 0.\nA19.5 With T fixed there is no averaging over time to wash out ūi ; all consistency comes from\nA19.6 ci cancels exactly; the transformed error üit is uncorrelated with every ẍis under strict exogeneity, so OLS on the transformed data is consistent.\nA19.7 Trace of IL is L, so rank(PZ ) = L; regressing x on Z uses L parameters; with K endogenous regressors the overidentification degree is L − K.\nA19.8 OLS: β + 0.5. IV with z: Cov(z, u) = 0 and Cov(z, x) = 1, so IV → β."}, {"pdf": 174, "printed": 147, "ch": 19, "text": "A19.9 Unit 1: ȳ = 2, x̄ = 1, so ÿ = (−1, 1), ẍ = (−1, 1). Unit 2: ȳ = 2.5, x̄ = 1.5, P 2 ÿ = (−0.5, 0.5), ẍ = (−0.5, 0.5). ẍÿ = (1 + 1) + (0.25 + 0.25) = 2.5; ẍ = (1 + 1) + (0.25 + 0.25) = 2.5. So β̂F E = 1.\nA19.10 Consistency tells you what the estimator is aiming at; it does not tell you the finitesample error. With N = 12 the asymptotic standard errors can be very misleading (Chapters 24–26); consistency is necessary, not sufficient.\nA19.11 For T = 2: ∆yi2 = yi2 − yi1 and ÿi2 = ∆yi2 /2, ÿi1 = −∆yi2 /2, so t ẍit ÿit = ∆xi ∆yi /2 P 2 and ẍit = ∆x2i /2: the ratio is the FD estimator. For T > 2 they differ: FD uses only adjacent differences and is serially correlated if uit is serially uncorrelated; FE is more efficient under serially uncorrelated u."}, {"pdf": 175, "printed": 148, "ch": 20, "text": "CHAPTER 20\n\nWooldridge, Chapter 5: Instrumental\nVariables and Two-Stage Least Squares\nSource line\nJeffrey M. Wooldridge, Econometric Analysis of Cross Section and Panel Data, 2nd ed. (MIT Press, 2010), Chapter 5, “Instrumental variables estimation of single-equation linear models”, printed pp. 83–113 (your file, 741 PDF pages, carries the printed page numbers in the running heads). I read sections 5.1–5.2 closely, including 5.2.6 on pitfalls; I did not work the end-of-chapter problems. The overidentification test, which Wooldridge puts in Section 6.2.2 (p. 122), is included here because it belongs with 2SLS; I mark where I go beyond Chapter 5.\n\n20.1 What problem does IV solve?\nWooldridge’s population model is y = β0 + β1 x1 + · · · + βK xK + u (5.1) with E(u) = 0 and Cov(xj , u) = 0 for j < K (5.2), but xK may be correlated with u (p. 83). OLS is then inconsistent for all the coefficients (p. 83), by Section 19.5 of Chapter 19. An instrument z1 must satisfy two conditions (pp. 83–84):\n1. Exogeneity: Cov(z1 , u) = 0 (5.3).\n2. Relevance: z1 is correlated with xK after partialling out the other exogenous regressors. In Wooldridge’s notation: write the linear projection xK = δ0 + δ1 x1 + · · · + δK−1 xK−1 + θ1 z1 + rK and require θ1 6= 0. Condition 1 cannot be tested when the model is exactly identified (Wooldridge says so explicitly); it is an assumption that must be argued from economics. Condition 2 can be tested by an F -test in the first-stage regression.\n\n20.2 The simple IV estimator: derivation\nTake one regressor with y = β0 + β1 x + u, instrument z. Subtract expectations and use Cov(z, u) = 0: Cov(z, y) = β1 Cov(z, x) + Cov(z, u) = β1 Cov(z, x)\n\nβ1 =\n\nCov(z, y)\nCov(z, x)\n\nReplacing population covariances by sample covariances gives β̂1,IV = (zi − z̄)(yi − ȳ)/ (zi − z̄)(xi − x̄). In general, with L = K instruments, the K population orthogonality conditions E(z ′ u) = 0 give β̂IV = (Z ′ X)−1 Z ′ y."}, {"pdf": 176, "printed": 149, "ch": 20, "text": "Sanity check with a binary instrument. If z ∈ {0, 1}, then β̂1,IV = (ȳ1 − ȳ0 )/(x̄1 − x̄0 ): the Wald estimator, the effect of z on y divided by the effect of z on x. This is the intuition to teach: IV rescales the “reduced-form” effect of z on y by the “first-stage” effect of z on x.\n\n20.3 Two-stage least squares (section 5.1.2, p. 90)\nWith more instruments (L > K) we must reduce the L instruments to K combinations. 2SLS does this by projecting each regressor on all of the instruments.\nStage 1: regress each xj on z (the full list of exogenous variables, including the exogenous regressors themselves), obtaining fitted values X̂ = PZ X.\nStage 2: regress y on X̂.\nResult 20.1 (2SLS closed form). The second-stage OLS coefficient equals\nβ̂ = X ′ PZ X\n\n\u0001−1\n\nX ′ PZ y,\n\nPZ = Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ .\n\nProof. Second-stage OLS is (X̂ ′ X̂)−1 X̂ ′ y with X̂ = PZ X. By Theorem 19.1, PZ is symmetric and idempotent, so X̂ ′ X̂ = X ′ PZ PZ X = X ′ PZ X and X̂ ′ y = X ′ PZ y. This is exactly equation (8.29)’s single-equation special case in Chapter 21: 2SLS is a GMM estimator with weighting matrix (Z ′ Z/N )−1 . Two traps (i) Standard errors. Running the second stage by hand gives the wrong standard errors. The correct residuals are ûi = yi − xi β̂ using the original xi (equation 5.25), not yi − x̂i β̂ (Wooldridge, p. 95). (ii) Forbidden regressions. The first stage must contain every exogenous regressor of the structural equation; leaving one out makes the second stage inconsistent.\n\n20.4 Consistency and asymptotic normality: the proofs\nAssumptions. 2SLS.1: E(z ′ u) = 0. 2SLS.2: (a) rank E(z ′ z) = L; (b) rank E(z ′ x) = K (the rank condition). 2SLS.3: E(u2 z ′ z) = σ 2 E(z ′ z). (Wooldridge, pp. 92–95.)\n\nResult 20.2 (Wooldridge Theorem 5.1: consistency). Under 2SLS.1 and 2SLS.2, β̂2SLS − → β. Proof. Write y = Xβ + u in the closed form of Theorem 20.1: β̂ = β +\n\nX′Z\n\n\u0001 Z ′ Z \u0001−1 Z ′ X \u0001i−1 X ′ Z \u0001 Z ′ Z \u0001−1 Z ′ u \u0001\n\nBy the WLLN, Z ′ X/N −\n→ C ≡ E(z ′ x), Z ′ Z/N −\n→ D ≡ E(z ′ z) and Z ′ u/N −\n→ E(z ′ u) = 0.\nThe bracket converges to C ′ D−1 C, which is invertible by 2SLS.2 (it is p.d. because C has full column rank K and D−1 is p.d.). Slutsky then gives β̂ − β − → (C ′ D−1 C)−1 C ′ D−1 · 0 = 0. ■"}, {"pdf": 177, "printed": 150, "ch": 20, "text": "Result 20.3 (Wooldridge Theorem 5.2:\nN (β̂ − β) −\n→ Normal 0, σ 2 (C ′ D−1 C)−1 .\n\nasymptotic normality). Under 2SLS.1–2SLS.3,\n\nProof. From the display above, N (β̂ − β) = [. . .]−1 (X ′ Z/N )(Z ′ Z/N )−1 N −1/2 Z ′ u. The summands zi′ ui have mean zero (2SLS.1) and variance E(u2 z ′ z) = σ 2 D (2SLS.3), so\n\nN −1/2 Z ′ u −\n→ Normal(0, σ 2 D) (CLT). Let A = (C ′ D−1 C)−1 C ′ D−1 . By Slutsky the\nlimit is Normal(0, σ 2 ADA′ ), and ADA′ = (C ′ D−1 C)−1 C ′ D−1 DD −1 C(C ′ D−1 C)−1 = (C ′ D−1 C)−1 . Wooldridge omits this proof (“similar to Theorem 4.2”); the algebra above is mine. The usual estimator of the variance replaces C, D by sample moments and σ 2 by σ̂ 2 = (N − K)−1 û2i (5.26). If 2SLS.3 fails, use the robust (sandwich) form of Section 5.2.5, as in OLS.\n\n20.5 Efficiency: why 2SLS is the best linear-instrument estimator\nResult 20.4 (Wooldridge Theorem 5.3). Under 2SLS.1–2SLS.3, 2SLS is asymptotically efficient in the class of IV estimators that use instruments linear in z. Proof. Let the competing estimator use instruments x̃ = zΓ (Γ an L × K constant matrix; rank condition assumed). Its asymptotic variance is σ 2 [E(x̃′ x)]−1 E(x̃′ x̃)[E(x′ x̃)]−1 . 2SLS uses x∗ = zΠ with Π = D−1 C, which is the linear projection of x on z; because x − x∗ is orthogonal to z, E(x̃′ x) = E(x̃′ x∗ ) for every x̃ = zΓ. Apply the matrix inequality (projection of x∗ onto x̃) E(x∗′ x̃) [E(x̃′ x̃)]−1 E(x̃′ x∗ ) \u0016 E(x∗′ x∗ ), which holds because the left side is the variance explained by projecting x∗ on x̃ and cannot exceed the total variance. Inverting reverses the inequality: Avar(β̃) = σ 2 [. . .]−1 \u0017 σ 2 [E(x∗′ x∗ )]−1 = Avar(β̂2SLS ). In words: among all ways of combining the instruments linearly, the combination that is the best linear predictor of the regressors wins. The condition 2SLS.3 (homoskedasticity) is what makes the best linear combination also the best use of the instruments. Under heteroskedasticity, GMM with a robust weighting matrix does better (Chapter 21).\n\n20.6 Weak instruments (section 5.2.6, pp. 101–105)\nThree facts Wooldridge stresses.\n1. IV is never unbiased when a regressor is endogenous. Wooldridge (p. 101, citing Kinal 1980) says that, under normality, how many moments 2SLS has is tied to the number of overidentifying restrictions, so that with L = K the estimator has no mean at all. (I have not rechecked the exact count in Kinal’s paper; textbooks differ by one in how they state it, so do not cite the count itself.) We rely on large-sample theory."}, {"pdf": 178, "printed": 151, "ch": 20, "text": "2. A small correlation between instrument and error is magnified by weak relevance. For a single regressor and instrument, plim β̂1,IV = β1 +\n\nCov(z1 , u)\nσu Corr(z1 , u)\n= β1 +\nCov(z1 , x1 )\nσx1 Corr(z1 , x1 )\n\n(5.36–5.37).\n\n(20.1)\n\nOLS has plim β̂OLS = β1 + (σu /σx1 )Corr(x1 , u). IV is worse than OLS in terms of inconsistency whenever |Corr(z1 , u)/Corr(z1 , x1 )| > |Corr(x1 , u)| (p. 102). c RK with SS c RK = SST [ K (1 − R̂2 ),\n3. Standard errors blow up. Avar(β̂K ) ≈ σ 2 /SS\n\nAsymptotic bias in units of σu/σx\n\n2 is the R2 of x̂\nwhere R̂K\nK on the other regressors (5.39–5.41, pp. 102–103). A weak\n2 close\ninstrument makes SST K (the total variation of the fitted values) small and R̂K to 1. Identification needs only that z enter the first stage with a non-zero coefficient; precision needs it to matter.\n\nIV: corr(z,u)=0.05 ÷ corr(z,x)\n\n1.4\n\nOLS: corr(x,u)=0.30\n\n1.2\n1.0\n0.8\nequal at corr(z,x)=1/6\n\n0.6\n0.4\n0.2\n0.0\n\n0.0\n\n0.1\n\n0.2\n0.3\n0.4\nInstrument strength, corr(z, x)\n\n0.5\n\n0.6\n\nFigure 20.1. Equation (20.1) in action (my chart). An instrument whose correlation with the error is only 0.05 is asymptotically worse than OLS (whose own correlation with the error is 0.30) once its correlation with the regressor falls below 0.05/0.30 = 1/6.\n\nWorked example (my numbers).\nCov(x, u) = 0.4, β = 2.\nCase\n\nPopulation:\n\nVar(x) = Var(u) = Var(z) = 1,\n\nCov(z, x) Cov(z, u) plim of IV\n\nOLS (benchmark)\n\n2 + 0.4/1 = 2.40\n\nStrong, valid instrument\n\n0.5\n\n2.00\n\nStrong, slightly invalid\n\n0.5\n\n0.05\n\n2 + 0.05/0.5 = 2.10\n\nWeak, slightly invalid\n\n0.1\n\n0.05\n\n2 + 0.05/0.1 = 2.50 (worse than OLS)\n\nBeyond Chapter 5 (flagged: general knowledge). Practitioners test relevance with the first-stage F statistic on the excluded instruments; the widely quoted rule of thumb is F > 10 for one endogenous regressor (Staiger and Stock 1997; Stock and Yogo 2005). I give the rule from general knowledge, not from the Wooldridge pages above, which stress the partial 2 instead. Check Stock and Yogo before citing a threshold. correlation and the role of R̂K"}, {"pdf": 179, "printed": 152, "ch": 20, "text": "20.7 Testing overidentifying restrictions (Wooldridge Section 6.2.2,\np. 122)\nIf L > K there are Q1 = L − K instruments more than needed. Each excess instrument gives a testable implication.\n1. Estimate by 2SLS; save the residuals ûi = yi − xi β̂.\n2. Regress ûi on all of zi ; let Ru2 be the (uncentred) R2 .\n\n3. Under the null that all instruments are exogenous and under 2SLS.3, N Ru2 − → χ2Q1 . Why it works (my sketch). If E(z ′ u) = 0 for all L instruments, the 2SLS residuals û must be orthogonal to z up to sampling error, except in the K directions that 2SLS has used up (because the normal equations force X̂ ′ û = 0). That leaves L − K free directions, hence χ2L−K . Chapter 21 gives the general and exact version (the J statistic). What a rejection means and does not mean. It says that some instrument is invalid or the model is misspecified; it does not say which. With a single endogenous regressor and several instruments it can be a sign that instruments have different effects (heterogeneity). A failure to reject says little if the instruments are weak or few, and as Chapter 25 shows, it says nothing at all if there are too many.\n\n20.8 Common misreadings\nFive things people get wrong\n\n1. “IV removes the bias.” It removes the inconsistency if the instrument is valid; in finite samples IV is biased and can be badly so.\n2. “A significant first stage is enough.” Relevance is necessary; exogeneity is an untestable assumption in the just-identified case.\n3. “Use as many instruments as possible.” More instruments give more asymptotic efficiency under homoskedasticity but raise finite-sample bias toward OLS.\n4. “The second-stage standard errors are correct.” They are not (equation 5.25).\n5. “Lagged values are automatically valid instruments.” They are valid only if the error is serially uncorrelated enough (Chapter 23).\n\n20.9 What it means for the thesis\nMy inference.\n• In a bank panel, climate-related variables (transition-risk exposure, carbon intensity of lending) are plausibly correlated with unobserved bank strategy captured in the error. Any claim that an instrument is exogenous has to be defended in the text; there is no test for it when the model is just identified."}, {"pdf": 180, "printed": 153, "ch": 20, "text": "• Weak-instrument arithmetic (Figure 20.1) is the first thing an examiner may check: report the first-stage F and the correlation between instrument and regressor.\n• With only 12 or so public-sector banks, instrument strength must be judged from the fit, not from asymptotic theory; Chapter 26 gives a small simulation of what happens in this size of panel. Do you need the original? For the derivations and the logic: no. The proofs above are complete and, for Theorem 5.2, fill in a step Wooldridge leaves out. Read pp. 101–105 in the original if you will teach weak instruments: the discussion 2 and SST [ K is short and good. of R̂K Read Section 5.3 (pp. 105–107) if your thesis proposes IV solutions to omitted variables or measurement error; I have not covered it. Check the Stock–Yogo threshold in its source.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ20.1 State the two conditions for a valid instrument and say which can be tested.\nQ20.2 Write the 2SLS estimator in matrix form.\nQ20.3 What residuals must be used to estimate σ 2 in 2SLS?\nQ20.4 State Theorem 5.3.\nExplain\nQ20.5 Show that 2SLS with one instrument and one regressor equals the simple IV estimator.\nQ20.6 Why is the first stage required to include all exogenous regressors?\nQ20.7 Why does Wooldridge say that IV has no finite mean when L = K?\nQ20.8 Explain, using (20.1), when IV is worse than OLS.\nApply\nQ20.9 Data: z = (0, 0, 1, 1), x = (1, 2, 4, 5), y = (2, 3, 7, 9).\nCov(z, y)/Cov(z, x) and the OLS slope.\n\nCompute the IV slope\n\nQ20.10 A regression of the capital ratio on a climate-exposure index uses the exposure of a bank’s state-owned parent as an instrument. Write down the two conditions and say what evidence would support each.\nQ20.11 Given Corr(z, u) = 0.1 and Corr(z, x) = 0.3, Corr(x, u) = 0.25: which is more inconsistent, IV or OLS? Viva\nQ20.12 “Instruments cannot be tested, so IV results are never credible.” Discuss.\nQ20.13 “An overidentification test that does not reject proves the instruments are valid.” Re153"}, {"pdf": 181, "printed": 154, "ch": 20, "text": "spond.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA20.1 Exogeneity (Cov(z, u) = 0), relevance (partial correlation with x). Relevance is testable.\nA20.2 β̂ = (X ′ PZ X)−1 X ′ PZ y.\nA20.3 ûi = yi − xi β̂ with the original xi .\nA20.4 2SLS is efficient among IV estimators with instruments linear in z, under 2SLS.1– 2SLS.3.\nA20.5 With L = K, X ′ Z is square and invertible, so\n(X ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ X)−1 = (Z ′ X)−1 (Z ′ Z)(X ′ Z)−1 ;\nmultiplying by X ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ y leaves (Z ′ X)−1 Z ′ y, the simple IV estimator.\nA20.6 Otherwise the second stage uses an x̂ built with fewer instruments than the model uses; the omitted exogenous variable stays in the error and is correlated with x̂, causing inconsistency.\nA20.7 Wooldridge, citing Kinal (1980): the existence of moments depends on the number of overidentifying restrictions; with L = K there are none, so the estimator has no expected value.\nA20.8 When |Corr(z, u)/Corr(z, x)| > |Corr(x, u)|.\nA20.9 z̄ groups: z = 0: x mean 1.5, y mean 2.5; z = 1: x mean 4.5, y mean 8. Wald: (8 − 2.5)/(4.5 − 1.5) = 5.5/3 = 1.833. OLS: x̄ = 3, ȳ = 5.25; (x − x̄)(y − ȳ) = (−2)(−3.25) + (−1)(−2.25) + (1)(1.75) + (2)(3.75) = 6.5 + 2.25 + 1.75 + 7.5 = 18; (x − x̄)2 = 10; OLS = 1.8.\nA20.10 Exogeneity: the parent’s exposure affects the capital ratio only through the bank’s own exposure (hard to defend: ownership may affect capital rules directly). Relevance: firststage F . Evidence: institutional detail, placebo tests, comparison of first-stage and reduced form.\nA20.11 IV: 0.1/0.3 = 0.333 (times σu /σx ); OLS: 0.25. IV is more inconsistent.\nA20.12 Not tested but not unarguable: one can argue from institutions, examine reduced forms, run placebo checks and sensitivity analysis. “Never credible” overstates; “never proved” is right.\nA20.13 No: failure to reject has low power, especially with weak or few instruments, and with too many instruments it is vacuous (Chapter 25)."}, {"pdf": 182, "printed": 155, "ch": 21, "text": "CHAPTER 21\n\nWooldridge, Chapter 8: System Instrumental Variables and GMM\nSource line\nWooldridge (2010), Chapter 8, “System estimation by instrumental variables”, printed pp. 183–207. I read Sections 8.1–8.3 (pp. 183–198) closely, and used 8.5 (testing, pp. 199– 202) through the way Chapter 14 refers to it. Section 8.6 (optimal instruments, p. 202) and Chapter 9 (simultaneous equations) are not covered. The proofs below follow Wooldridge’s statements; where he writes “we will not prove this”, the algebra is mine.\n\n21.1 Why a system?\nA panel with T periods is a system of T equations, one per period, for each cross-section unit i (pp. 183–186). So are SUR models and simultaneous equations. Wooldridge’s general linear system is yi = X i β + u i , (21.1) where yi and ui are G × 1 and Xi is G × K, β is K × 1 and the data are i.i.d. across i (equation 8.11). A G × L matrix of instruments Zi supplies the identifying information. Two assumptions:\n• SIV.1: E(Zi′ ui ) = 0 (the L orthogonality conditions).\n• SIV.2 (rank condition): rank E(Zi′ Xi ) = K.\nDynamic panels are the application that matters for you. Setting G = T − 1 (first differences) and letting row t of Zi contain different instruments at different dates (the matrix in Wooldridge’s equation 11.16, Chapter 23) turns Anderson–Hsiao, Arellano–Bond and Blundell– Bond into special cases of this chapter.\n\n21.2 The GMM estimator: derivation\nUnder SIV.1 and SIV.2, β is the unique solution of the population moment equation E[Zi′ (yi − Xi β)] = 0\n\n(21.2)\n\n(8.19).\n\nUniqueness: if b 6= β, then E[Zi′ (yi − Xi b)] = E(Zi′ Xi )(β − b) 6= 0 because E(Zi′ Xi ) has full column rank (equation 8.20). Case L = K. Replace the expectation by the sample average: N −1 giving β̂ = (Z ′ X)−1 Z ′ Y (8.22), the system IV estimator. 155\n\nZi′ (yi − Xi β̂) = 0,"}, {"pdf": 183, "printed": 156, "ch": 21, "text": "Case L > K. The L sample equations N −1 Zi′ (yi − Xi b) = 0 generally have no exact solution. Choose b to make them small in a quadratic form with a weighting matrix Ŵ (L × L, symmetric, p.s.d.): i′ h X (8.23). (21.3) β̂ = arg min Zi′ (yi − Xi b) Ŵ Zi′ (yi − Xi b)\n\nBy the first-order condition for a quadratic form (Chapter 19), the closed form is β̂ = (X ′ Z Ŵ Z ′ X)−1 (X ′ Z Ŵ Z ′ Y )\n\n(21.4)\n\n(8.24).\n\nDifferent Ŵ give different estimators, all consistent. When L = K, X ′ Z is square and invertible and Ŵ cancels.\n\nAssumption SIV.3: Ŵ −\n→ W , a constant, symmetric, p.d. matrix.\n\nResult 21.1 (Wooldridge Theorem 8.1: consistency). Under SIV.1–SIV.3, β̂ −\n→ β.\nProof. Substitute Y = Xβ + u into (21.4):\n\u0003−1\nβ̂ = β + (X ′ Z/N )Ŵ (Z ′ X/N ) (X ′ Z/N )Ŵ (Z ′ u/N ).\n\nLet C = E(Zi′ Xi ). The WLLN gives Z ′ X/N −\n→ C and Z ′ u/N −\n→ E(Zi′ ui ) = 0. The matrix\nC ′ W C is p.d. because C has rank K and W is p.d. By Slutsky, β̂ − β −\n→ (C ′ W C)−1 C ′ W · 0 =\n0.\nResult 21.2 (Wooldridge Theorem 8.2: asymptotic normality). Let Λ = E(Zi′ ui u′i Zi ) (equation 8.26). Under SIV.1–SIV.3, N (β̂ − β) − → Normal(0, VW ) with VW = (C ′ W C)−1 C ′ W ΛW C(C ′ W C)−1\n\n(8.25).\n\nProof. From the display in Theorem 21.1,\nN (β̂ − β) = [(X ′ Z/N )Ŵ (Z ′ X/N )]−1 (X ′ Z/N )Ŵ N −1/2 Zi′ ui .\nThe vectors Zi′ ui are i.i.d., have mean zero and variance Λ, so the CLT gives N −1/2 Zi′ ui − Normal(0, Λ). Slutsky and the rule Var(Aq) = A Var(q)A′ with A = (C ′ W C)−1 C ′ W give the result. (Wooldridge states this and omits the proof.) A consistent estimate is obtained by replacing C with Z ′ X/N , W with Ŵ and Λ with Λ̂ = N −1 Zi′ ûi û′i Zi , where ûi = yi − Xi β̃ uses residuals from any consistent preliminary estimator (equation 8.27).\n\n21.3 System 2SLS\n\u0001−1\nChoosing Ŵ = (Z ′ Z/N )−1 = N −1 Zi′ Zi\n(8.28) gives\n\u0003−1 ′\nβ̂ = X ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ X\nX Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ Y\n\n(8.29),\n\nwhich has the form of single-equation 2SLS (Theorem 20.1) with PZ built from the stacked Z. It requires no estimate of the error covariance and is therefore robust, but it is generally inefficient."}, {"pdf": 184, "printed": 157, "ch": 21, "text": "21.4 The optimal weighting matrix\nResult 21.3 (Wooldridge Theorem 8.3). Under SIV.1–SIV.3, with W = Λ−1 (assumption SIV.4), the asymptotic variance is Vopt = (C ′ Λ−1 C)−1 and VW − Vopt is p.s.d. for every p.d. Proof. (The following proof is mine.) Put A = (C ′ W C)−1 C ′ W and B = (C ′ Λ−1 C)−1 C ′ Λ−1 (K × L matrices), so VW = AΛA′ and Vopt = BΛB ′ . Compute BΛA′ = (C ′ Λ−1 C)−1 C ′ Λ−1 ΛW C(C ′ W C)−1 = (C ′ Λ−1 C)−1 = BΛB ′ . Therefore (A − B)Λ(A − B)′ = AΛA′ − AΛB ′ − BΛA′ + BΛB ′ = VW − Vopt − Vopt + Vopt = VW − Vopt , using AΛB ′ = (BΛA′ )′ = Vopt (Vopt is symmetric). The left-hand side is of the form DΛD′ with Λ p.s.d., hence p.s.d. Therefore VW \u0017 Vopt . In words: give less weight to moments that are noisier and to those that are redundant with others. The optimal weighting matrix is the inverse of the variance of the moments.\n\nAsymptotic variance of θ̂θ̂\n\n4.0\n\nAvar with W = diag(w, 1−w)\nOptimal: 1/(G′Λ⁻¹G) = 0.94\n\n3.5\n3.0\n2.5\n2.0\n1.5\n1.0\n0.0\n\n0.2\n\n0.4\n0.6\nWeight w on the first moment\n\n0.8\n\n1.0\n\nFigure 21.1. A one-parameter, two-moment example (my numbers): G = C = (1, 1)′ , 1 0.5 . Weighting the moments equally (w = 0.5, which is the same as W = I) gives an\n0.5 4\nasymptotic variance of 1.50; the optimum 1/(G′ Λ−1 G) = 0.9375 puts weight ≈ 0.88 on the precise first moment.\n\nFeasible two-step GMM (Wooldridge, p. 193).\n1. Estimate by system 2SLS (any consistent Ŵ ); compute residuals ûi .\n2. Form Λ̂ = N −1 Zi′ ûi û′i Zi and set Ŵ = Λ̂−1 .\n3. Re-estimate (21.4). Asymptotic variance: (X ′ Z/N Λ̂−1 Z ′ X/N )−1 /N ."}, {"pdf": 185, "printed": 158, "ch": 21, "text": "21.5 Three-stage least squares and the homoskedasticity case\nIf the system homoskedasticity assumption SIV.5 holds, E(Zi′ ui u′i Zi ) = E(Zi′ ΩZi ) with Ω = E(ui ui′ ), then Λ̂ = N −1 Zi′ Ω̂Zi , and the efficient GMM estimator is the (GMM) three-stage least squares estimator, built from system-2SLS residuals Ω̂ = N −1 ûi û′i (pp. 194–198, Theorem 8.4). With the same instruments in every equation, Zi = IG ⊗ zi , 3SLS collapses to equation-by-equation 2SLS when there are no cross-equation restrictions. Wooldridge warns (pp. 196–198) that the traditional 3SLS formula, which first transforms the instruments, differs from GMM 3SLS when instruments differ across equations, and in Section 11.1.1 that the traditional form is not consistent when the instruments are sequentially, rather than strictly, exogenous (p. 305). That is the case of dynamic panels.\n\n21.6 Testing\nOveridentifying restrictions (Section 8.5.2, p. 201).\nΛ̂−1 ) and the statistic\n\nTake the optimal estimator (Ŵ =\n\nJ = N N −1\nZi′ ûi Λ̂−1 N −1\nZi′ ûi = ĝ ′ Λ̂−1 ĝ,\n\n(21.5)\n\nwhich is the minimised value of the GMM objective. Under the null that all L moment conditions hold, J − → χ2L−K (Wooldridge’s equation 8.49). Derivation (mine). Let ĝ = N −1/2 Zi′ (yi − Xi β̂) and s = N −1/2 Zi′ ui − → Normal(0, Λ). Since ĝ = s − (N −1 Zi′ Xi ) N (β̂ − β) and the optimal estimator has N (β̂ − β) = (C ′ Λ−1 C)−1 C ′ Λ−1 s + op (1), we have ĝ = M s + op (1) with M = I − C(C ′ Λ−1 C)−1 C ′ Λ−1 . Write s = Λ1/2 ξ with ξ ∼ Normal(0, IL ) and C̃ = Λ−1/2 C. Then Λ−1/2 ĝ = (I−C̃(C̃ ′ C̃)−1 C̃ ′ )ξ = MC̃ ξ, and J = kΛ−1/2 ĝk2 = ξ ′ MC̃ ξ. MC̃ is a symmetric idempotent matrix of rank L − K (Theorem 19.1), so J ∼ χ2L−K . Reading the result: the first K directions in moment space are used up in estimating β; only the remaining L − K directions are free to disagree with zero. Testing a subset of the moments (the “difference-in-J” test). Wooldridge’s Section 8.5.1 gives the distance (criterion-function) statistic (8.45): estimate with all L moments and with L1 < L of them, using the same weighting matrix, and compare the minimised objectives; the difference is χ2L−L1 under the null that the extra moments are valid. This is the test used in Chapters 24–25 to check the extra moment conditions of system GMM. Wald tests. 8.48).\n\nFor H0 : Rβ = r, W = (Rβ̂ − r)′ [RV̂ R′ /N ]−1 (Rβ̂ − r) ∼ χ2rank(R) (equation\n\n21.7 Choosing an estimator (Section 8.4, p. 198)"}, {"pdf": 186, "printed": 159, "ch": 21, "text": "Estimator\n\nWhen it is efficient\n\nMain risk\n\nSystem 2SLS\n\nNever optimal in general; fine as a ro- Inefficient under heteroskedasticity or bust first step serial correlation\n\nGMM 3SLS (SIV.5)\n\nUnder system homoskedasticity\n\nOptimal\ntwo-step Always, asymptotically\nGMM (Λ̂−1 )\n\nInconsistent variance estimates if SIV.5\nfails\nΛ̂ has many elements; estimated imprecisely when L is large relative to N (the key warning of Chapters 24–25)\n\nTable 21.1. Weighting-matrix choices. The last row is where small-sample trouble comes from: you are estimating an L × L matrix from N clusters.\n\n21.8 Common misreadings\nFour things people get wrong\n\n1. “GMM is a different method from IV.” IV, 2SLS, 3SLS and system IV are GMM\nwith particular weighting matrices.\n2. “The efficient GMM estimator is always best.” It is best asymptotically; in finite samples the estimated Λ̂−1 adds noise, and system 2SLS can win.\n3. “The J test has L degrees of freedom.” It has L − K.\n4. “J fails to reject, so the model is right.” It is a test of the overidentifying restrictions only; exact identification is untestable.\n\n21.9 What it means for the thesis\nMy inference. Everything you will do with dynamic panel GMM (Chapters 23–25) is an instance of equations (21.3)–(21.5); the only new ingredient is which columns go into Zi . If you can write down Zi for your model you can read any xtabond2 or pgmm output: the table shows β̂, standard errors from (C ′ Λ̂−1 C)−1 /N , the number of instruments L and the J statistic with L − K degrees of freedom. Do you need the original? For the logic and proofs of Theorems 8.1–8.3 and the J test: no. Read Section 8.3.5 (pp. 196–198, GMM 3SLS versus traditional 3SLS) in the original if you intend to estimate with a system routine rather than a dynamic-panel command. Read Section 8.6 (optimal instruments) only if you plan to use nonlinear efficiency arguments; I did not read it.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ21.1 State SIV.1 and SIV.2."}, {"pdf": 187, "printed": 160, "ch": 21, "text": "Q21.2 What is the GMM estimator in closed form for a given Ŵ ? What is the optimal Ŵ ?\nQ21.3 What is the null distribution of J, and its degrees of freedom?\nExplain\nQ21.4 Why is the GMM estimator consistent for any p.d. W ?\nQ21.5 Explain why Ŵ = (Z ′ Z/N )−1 gives 2SLS.\nQ21.6 Why does the optimal weighting matrix depend on the variance of the moments, not of the errors?\nQ21.7 Why can adding moment conditions never reduce asymptotic efficiency (with the optimal weight), yet hurt in small samples? Apply\nQ21.8 Compute the asymptotic variance for W =!I and for W = Λ−1 in the example of 2 1 Figure 21.1, with G = (1, 1)′ and Λ = 1 2\nQ21.9 A dynamic panel model has 14 moment conditions and 3 parameters. What are the degrees of freedom of J? If J = 26, what is the decision at 5%?\nQ21.10 Suppose L = K. What is J? Why?\nViva\nQ21.11 “Because two-step GMM is efficient, always use it.” Respond.\nQ21.12 “GMM requires fewer assumptions than maximum likelihood, so it is always preferable.” Discuss.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA21.1 E(Zi′ ui ) = 0; rank E(Zi′ Xi ) = K.\n\nA21.2 β̂ = (X ′ Z Ŵ Z ′ X)−1 X ′ Z Ŵ Z ′ Y ; Ŵ −\n→ Λ−1 .\nA21.3 χ2L−K .\nA21.4 Because the sample moments converge to zero at the truth and C ′ W C is invertible for any p.d. W .\nA21.5 Substituting Ŵ = (Z ′ Z/N )−1 in (21.4) gives [X ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ X]−1 X ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ Y , the 2SLS formula.\nA21.6 Because the estimator is built from the moments; the precision of a moment, not of an error, decides its weight.\nA21.7 With the optimal weight an extra moment can be given weight zero, so asymptotic variance cannot rise; in finite samples Λ̂ must be estimated with more elements and the resulting noise biases the estimate.\nA21.8 W = I: G′ ΛG = (1, 1)Λ(1, 1)′ = 2 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 6, G′ G = 2, so V = 6/4 = 1.5. Optimal:"}, {"pdf": 188, "printed": 161, "ch": 21, "text": "2 −1\n, G′ Λ−1 G = 13 (2 − 1 − 1 + 2) = 23 , so V = 1.5. Equal: here the\n−1 2\nmoments are exchangeable, so equal weights are already optimal.\n\nΛ−1 = 13\n\nA21.9 14 − 3 = 11; the 5% critical value of χ211 is 19.68; 26 > 19.68, reject.\nA21.10 J = 0: the moment equations can be solved exactly, so the minimised objective is zero.\nA21.11 Efficiency is asymptotic; with many moments Λ̂ is poorly estimated, the standard errors from two-step GMM are too small, and bias grows (Chapters 24–25).\nA21.12 GMM needs only moment conditions, so it is robust to distributional misspecification, but it is less efficient when the likelihood is correct and, with many moments, it can behave badly in small samples."}, {"pdf": 189, "printed": 162, "ch": 22, "text": "CHAPTER 22\n\nWooldridge, Chapter 14: Generalized\nMethod of Moments and Minimum Distance\nSource line\nWooldridge (2010), Chapter 14, “Generalized method of moments and minimum distance estimation”. I read Sections 14.1–14.2 (the general nonlinear GMM set-up, consistency, asymptotic normality, optimal weighting, Lemma 14.1) and 14.4 (the J statistic and the panel Euler-equation application). I did not read the minimum-distance section (14.6) in detail. Equation numbers are Wooldridge’s; page numbers are not given here because I did not record them reliably, so check the original if you need to cite a page. All proofs written out below are my own working from his statements.\n\n22.1 Why Chapter 8 was not enough\nChapter 21 dealt with moment conditions that are linear in the parameters: E[Zi′ (yi −Xi β)] = 0. Many models give moments that are nonlinear: an Euler equation of consumption, a model with a nonlinear regression function, or, importantly, a panel model whose moment conditions are written in terms of a parameter vector θ that enters both equations and instruments. The general statement is E[g(wi , θ0 )] = 0, (22.1) where wi is the data vector for unit i, g is L × 1, and θ0 is the P × 1 true parameter. The sample counterpart is ḡN (θ) = N −1 i g(wi , θ). The estimator minimises a quadratic form: θ̂ = arg min ḡN (θ)′ Ŵ ḡN (θ) θ∈Θ\n\n(14.1).\n\n(22.2)\n\n22.2 Consistency\nResult 22.1 (Consistency, Wooldridge Theorem 14.1 in outline). Suppose (i) Θ is compact; (ii) g(w, θ) is continuous in θ and E[supθ kg(w, θ)k] < ∞; (iii) Ŵ − → W p.d.; (iv) identification: E[g(w, θ)] = 0 only at θ = θ0 . Then θ̂ − → θ0 .\n\nSketch (mine). By a uniform law of large numbers (condition ii), supθ kḡN (θ) − E g(w, θ)k − → 0. So the sample objective converges uniformly to Q(θ) = E[g(w, θ)]′ W E[g(w, θ)], which is ≥ 0 and equals zero only at θ0 (iv). A continuous function minimised uniquely on a compact set: any estimator that minimises the sample objective converges to that minimiser. (This is"}, {"pdf": 190, "printed": 163, "ch": 22, "text": "the standard extremum-estimator argument; Wooldridge cites Newey and McFadden for the general proof.) What to retain: (iv) is the only substantive condition, and it is the nonlinear analogue of the rank condition in Chapter 20. In nonlinear models it can fail in ways that a rank check does not reveal (multiple roots of the moment equations), which is why local identification (a rank condition on the Jacobian) is checked in practice.\n\n22.3 Asymptotic normality: the mean-value expansion\nDefine the Jacobian G(θ) = E[∇θ g(w, θ)], L × P , and G0 = G(θ0 ), and let Λ = E[g(w, θ0 )g(w, θ0 )′ ]. Assume G0 has rank P . Result 22.2 (Asymptotic normality, equations 14.8–14.10). Under the regularity conditions, N (θ̂ − θ0 ) − → Normal(0, VW ), where VW = (G′0 W G0 )−1 G′0 W ΛW G0 (G′0 W G0 )−1 . Derivation (mine, following Wooldridge’s route). The first-order condition of (22.2) is Ĝ′ Ŵ ḡN (θ̂) = 0,\n\nĜ = ∇θ ḡN (θ̂).\n\nExpand ḡN (θ̂) about θ0 with the mean-value theorem: ḡN (θ̂) = ḡN (θ0 ) + ḠN (θ̄)(θ̂ − θ0 ) where θ̄ lies between θ̂ and θ0 and ḠN is evaluated there (row by row). Substitute into the first-order condition: Ĝ′ Ŵ ḡN (θ0 ) + Ĝ′ Ŵ ḠN (θ̄)(θ̂ − θ0 ) = 0 \u0003−1 ⇒ N (θ̂ − θ0 ) = − Ĝ′ Ŵ ḠN (θ̄) Ĝ′ Ŵ N ḡN (θ0 ). By consistency, Ĝ − → G0 and ḠN (θ̄) − → G0 . The CLT gives N ḡN (θ0 ) − → Normal(0, Λ). Slutsky then gives a limit of −(G′0 W G0 )−1 G′0 W · Normal(0, Λ), with the stated variance. Link to Chapter 8: if g(w, θ) = Z ′ (y − Xθ), then G0 = −C and the formula collapses to VW of Theorem 21.2.\n\n22.4 Optimal weighting and Lemma 14.1\nResult 22.3 (Optimal weight, Wooldridge Theorem 14.2). With W = Λ−1 the asymptotic variance is (G′0 Λ−1 G0 )−1 , and VW − (G′0 Λ−1 G0 )−1 is p.s.d. for every W . The proof is that of Theorem 21.3 with C replaced by G0 (the cross term BΛA′ again equals BΛB ′ ), so I do not repeat it. Wooldridge presents the result via his Lemma 14.1, an algebraic fact on which the efficiency proofs of several estimators rest."}, {"pdf": 191, "printed": 164, "ch": 22, "text": "Lemma 14.1 in my words (proof mine)\n\nLet A and B be matrices with A = B + D where DΛB ′ = 0 (“the cross term vanishes”). Then AΛA′ − BΛB ′ = DΛD′ , which is p.s.d. So the variance of A-based estimator is never smaller than that of the B-based one. Recipe for efficiency proofs: show that the difference between a candidate and the efficient estimator is uncorrelated with the efficient one. This is the Hausman principle; it is also why the efficient estimator’s variance is the benchmark. Check: AΛA′ = (B + D)Λ(B + D)′ = BΛB ′ + BΛD′ + DΛB ′ + DΛD′ , and the cross terms are zero by assumption.\n\n22.5 The J statistic for nonlinear moments\nWith Ŵ = Λ̂−1 the minimised objective, scaled, is\nJ = N ḡN (θ̂)′ Λ̂−1 ḡN (θ̂) −\n→ χ2L−P\n\n(14.x).\n\nThe proof is the one in Chapter 21: the mean-value expansion gives N ḡN (θ̂) ≈ [I − G0 (G′0 Λ−1 G0 )−1 G′0 Λ−1 ] N ḡN (θ0 ), a rank-(L − P ) projection of a normal vector. (I could not match the equation number of this statement in the book; I mark it “x”.)\n\nWhat the J test can and cannot do\n• It tests the over-identifying restrictions jointly. A rejection says that at least one moment is invalid (or the model is misspecified); it does not say which.\n• It has low power against some failures. It cannot detect a violation that the parameters can “absorb”, and under exact identification it is zero.\n• With many moments and small N it does not behave as the theory says. My\nown simulations (below) show that with 103 moment conditions and 12 cross-section units the statistic is practically always far below its critical value, so its p-value is close to one. Configuration GMM, α = 0.5)\n\n(SYS-\n\ndf Mean p Share p > 0.9 Share p < 0.05\n\nN = 200, T = 6, all lags\n\n0.49\n\n4.7%\n\nN = 12, T = 15, all lags\n\n103\n\n1.00\n\n100%\n\nN = 12, T = 15, 2 lags\n\n1.00\n\n100%\n\nN = 12, T = 15, collapsed\n\n0.48\n\nTable 22.1. My Monte Carlo (500 replications each) of the Hansen/Sargan p-value when the model is correct. With a healthy sample the p-values are close to uniform (mean about 0.5, 5% rejections). With many instruments and few units the test never rejects, and so tells you nothing. Collapsing the instruments restores sensible behaviour."}, {"pdf": 192, "printed": 165, "ch": 22, "text": "22.6 Application: panel Euler equations and sequential moments\nAn important use in Wooldridge’s Chapter 14 is panel data models in which the error is a forecast error (an Euler equation, e.g. for consumption or capital): the condition is E[rit (θ) | Ii,t−1 ] = 0, so any variable dated t−1 or earlier is a valid instrument. For each t the instrument set therefore grows with t, and the vector of moments has the staircase form that reappears as the Arellano–Bond instrument matrix (Chapter 23). The statistical lesson transfers directly: sequential exogeneity gives many moments, and the weighting matrix Λ̂ with L(L+1)/2 distinct elements must be estimated from N units.\n\n22.7 Common misreadings\nMisreadings\n\n1. “Nonlinear GMM is more complicated in kind.” It is the same logic, with a Jacobian G0 taking the place of −C.\n2. “Consistency needs the optimal weight.” It does not; any p.d. limit works. Only efficiency needs Λ−1 .\n3. “A large J p-value is reassuring.” It can be a symptom of too many instruments (Table 22.1).\n4. “GMM variance formulas hold in small samples.” They are asymptotic; two-step standard errors are known to be too small when L is large.\n\n22.8 What it means for the thesis\nMy inference. You will mainly need the general principle, not the nonlinear machinery: (i) a GMM estimate is characterised by (Z, Ŵ ); (ii) its standard error is only as reliable as Λ̂; (iii) the J test must be read together with the number of instruments. With 12 banks, any specification whose instrument count exceeds 12 should be accompanied by a robustness check with collapsed or lag-limited instruments. Do you need the original? For the logic of nonlinear GMM, the expansion proof and the J statistic: no. Read the original only for regularity conditions (the precise assumptions in Theorems 14.1–14.2) or if you plan nonlinear moments such as Euler equations. Check page numbers there.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ22.1 Write the GMM objective for general moments g(w, θ).\nQ22.2 What is the Jacobian G0 in a linear IV model?"}, {"pdf": 193, "printed": 166, "ch": 22, "text": "Explain\nQ22.3 Why is the mean-value theorem needed to derive the asymptotic distribution?\nQ22.4 What does the vanishing cross term in Lemma 14.1 mean statistically?\nQ22.5 Why is the J test uninformative when L is large relative to N ?\nApply\nQ22.6 Show that for g = Z ′ (y −Xθ) the formula VW in Theorem 22.2 reduces to Theorem 21.2.\nQ22.7 A model has 60 moments and 4 parameters. Degrees of freedom of J? How many distinct elements does Λ have? Viva\nQ22.8 “Identification in GMM is just a rank condition.” Discuss for nonlinear models.\nQ22.9 Why is a high J p-value in a panel of 12 banks not good news?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA22.1 θ̂ = arg min ḡN (θ)′ Ŵ ḡN (θ).\nA22.2 G0 = −E(Zi′ Xi ).\nA22.3 The estimator is defined implicitly by a first-order condition nonlinear in θ̂; the expansion linearises it around θ0 so that the CLT applies to a sum.\nA22.4 The efficient estimator’s error is uncorrelated with the difference, so any inefficient estimator equals the efficient one plus orthogonal noise.\nA22.5 The weighting matrix is poorly estimated and the sample moments can be fitted almost perfectly, so the minimised objective is too small.\nA22.6 With G0 = −C, VW = (C ′ W C)−1 C ′ W ΛW C(C ′ W C)−1 ; the sign cancels in the sandwich.\nA22.7 60 − 4 = 56; 60 · 61/2 = 1830 distinct elements.\nA22.8 Local identification needs the Jacobian to have full column rank, but global identification may fail through multiple solutions; the rank check cannot detect that.\nA22.9 With few units and many instruments the test has almost no power; see Table 22.1."}, {"pdf": 194, "printed": 167, "ch": 23, "text": "CHAPTER 23\n\nDynamic Panel Data: Nickell Bias,\nAnderson–Hsiao and Arellano–Bond\nSource line\nWooldridge (2010), Chapter 11 (“More on efficient estimation and GLS”; the dynamicpanel parts are Section 11.6, “Panel data models with unobserved effects and sequential exogeneity”), with Section 10.2 for the static panel background. The derivations of the Nickell bias and of the instrument counts are mine, worked out from the model; where I cite Wooldridge’s equation numbers (11.16 for the Arellano–Bond instrument matrix) I did so from my reading notes. The Nickell, Anderson–Hsiao and Arellano–Bond papers themselves are not in the folder; I describe their content from standard knowledge and flag it as such.\n\n23.1 The model and the problem\nThe simplest dynamic panel model with an unobserved effect is\nyit = ρ yi,t−1 + ci + uit ,\n\nt = 1, . . . , T,\n\n|ρ| < 1,\n\n(23.1)\n\nwhere ci is a bank-specific effect and uit is idiosyncratic. Assume E(uit | yi0 , . . . , yi,t−1 , ci ) = 0 and uit serially uncorrelated (sequential exogeneity). Three obvious estimators fail:\n• Pooled OLS ignores ci , but yi,t−1 contains ci , so Cov(yi,t−1 , ci + uit ) > 0: the estimate of ρ is biased upward.\n• Fixed effects (within) removes ci but creates a new correlation, shown next, biased downward.\n• First differences with OLS: ∆yit = ρ∆yi,t−1 + ∆uit and ∆yi,t−1 contains ui,t−1 , which is in ∆uit . Biased (downward). Lagged y is never strictly exogenous, which is why the strict-exogeneity result for FE (Theorem 19.3) does not apply here.\n\n23.2 Nickell bias: derivation\nLet T be the number of observations per unit, yi1 , . . . , yiT , so that the regression has S = T − 1 usable periods. (If you count regression periods instead, replace T − 1 by T below.) Write dotted variables as deviations from unit means. The within estimator is P P i P t ÿi,t−1 üit ρ̂F E − ρ = P t ÿi,t−1"}, {"pdf": 195, "printed": 168, "ch": 23, "text": "Index lagged values yi,s−1 , s = 1, . . . , S (I drop i).\nNumerator. Since t (yt−1 − ȳ−1 )ū = 0, we have t ÿt−1 üt = t yt−1 ut − S ȳ−1 ū. The first sum has expectation zero (sequential exogeneity). For the second, E[ys−1 ut ] = ρs−1−t σu2 if t ≤ s − 1 and 0 otherwise, so S s−1 σu2 h 1 X X s−1−t 2 1 − ρS i σu = E S ȳ−1 ū = S(1 − ρ) 1−ρ s=1 t=1\n\nFor large S this is about σu2 /(1 − ρ) (the term (1 − ρS )/[S(1 − ρ)] → 0). So the numerator has expectation ≈ −σu2 /(1 − ρ) per unit, and it does not grow with S (each additional period adds a lagged variable, but the correlation with the demeaning term shrinks at the same rate). Denominator. In the stationary case Var(yt−1 | c) = σu2 /(1−ρ2 ), so the within sum of squares per unit is ≈ Sσu2 /(1 − ρ2 ). Ratio. plimN →∞ (ρ̂F E − ρ) ≈\n\n1+ρ\n1+ρ\n−σu2 /(1 − ρ)\nSσu2 /(1 − ρ2 )\nT −1\n\nApproximate bias of the within estimator\n\n(Nickell’s exact formula adds correction factors in ρT that vanish quickly. I did not verify them against the paper.) 0.0\n\n−0.2\n\nsquares: my simulation\nat T=15 (N=12)\n\n−0.4\n\n−0.6\n\n−0.8\n\nρ = 0.5: −(1+ρ)/(T−1)\nρ = 0.8: −(1+ρ)/(T−1)\n\nNumber of periods T\n\nFigure 23.1. The first-order within-estimator bias −(1 + ρ)/(T − 1) against T , for ρ = 0.5 and 0.8. Squares mark my own Monte Carlo with 12 units and T = 15: −0.123 (true ρ = 0.5) and −0.166 (ρ = 0.8), against the formula’s −0.107 and −0.129. The formula is first-order only; it understates the bias at small T and high ρ.\n\nKey points. (a) The bias is of order 1/T , not 1/N : adding banks does not fix it; (b) it is larger when ρ is larger; (c) at T = 15 it is still about −0.1 to −0.17, i.e. not negligible; (d) with the OLS bias (upward) and the FE bias (downward) one obtains a bracket for any consistent estimate (Chapter 25).\n\n23.3 First differences and Anderson–Hsiao\nDifferencing (23.1) removes ci :\n∆yit = ρ ∆yi,t−1 + ∆uit ,\n168\n\nt = 2, . . . , T.\n\n(23.2)"}, {"pdf": 196, "printed": 169, "ch": 23, "text": "∆yi,t−1 = yi,t−1 − yi,t−2 is correlated with ∆uit = uit − ui,t−1 through ui,t−1 . The instrument yi,t−2 is uncorrelated with ∆uit (it depends only on ui,t−2 and earlier) provided u has no serial correlation. This is the Anderson–Hsiao (1981) estimator (from standard knowledge, not checked): 2SLS on (23.2) with yi,t−2 (or ∆yi,t−2 ) as the single instrument. It is consistent but inefficient (it uses one moment per period although more are valid) and it can be weak (Chapter 24).\n\n23.4 Arellano–Bond: all valid lags\nAt period t, every yis with s ≤ t − 2 is uncorrelated with ∆uit : E[yis ∆uit ] = 0 for s = 0, . . . , t −\n2. Stack these in a G × L instrument matrix (Wooldridge’s equation 11.16 for sequentially exogenous regressors) whose row t contains (yi0 , . . . , yi,t−2 ) in its own block and zeros elsewhere: yi0 ··· · · ·  0 yi0 yi1 Zi =  0 In Chapter 21 notation, yi → ∆yi , Xi → ∆yi,−1 , ui → ∆ui and GMM with Ŵ = Λ̂−1 applies unchanged. This is Arellano–Bond (1991) difference GMM. Number of moments. With T observations yi1 , . . . , yiT (so differenced equations exist for t = 3, . . . , T and the instruments for t are y1 , . . . , yt−2 ), the count is\n\n(t − 2) =\n\nt=3\n\n(T − 1)(T − 2)\n\nFor T = 15 this is 91, the number reported in my simulations. It grows quadratically in T . Difference GMM, all lags: (T−1)(T−2)/2\n\nNumber of instruments\n\nSystem GMM, all lags: + (T−2)\nDifference GMM, 2 lags only\n\n102\n\nCollapsed, 2 lags\n\n101\n\nNumber of periods T\n\nFigure 23.2. Number of instruments against T for four instrument policies. The “all lags” counts for difference and system GMM grow quadratically; restricting to two lags makes growth linear; collapsing keeps a count independent of T . With 12 banks, any line above 12 is a warning (Chapter 25)."}, {"pdf": 197, "printed": 170, "ch": 23, "text": "Serial correlation test. In differences, ∆uit and ∆ui,t−1 share ui,t−1 , so first-order serial correlation (AR(1)) is expected. What matters is the absence of second-order (AR(2)): if it is present then yi,t−2 is correlated with ∆uit and the instruments are invalid. Hence the usual report: AR(1) rejected, AR(2) not rejected, Hansen J not rejected (Chapter 25).\n\n23.5 When difference GMM is weak\nIf ρ is close to one, yi,t−2 is almost uninformative about ∆yi,t−1 ; the series is nearly a random walk and the differences are close to unpredictable from levels. The first-stage fit is derived in Chapter 24. In my simulations at N = 200, T = 4 the difference estimator for ρ = 0.9 has a mean of 0.405 and a standard deviation of 0.78 (Table in Chapter 24): it is consistent but useless in finite samples.\n\n23.6 Common misreadings\nMisreadings\n\n1. “Fixed effects solves the dynamic panel problem.” No: it trades an upward bias for a downward bias of order 1/T .\n2. “Nickell bias disappears with large N .” It disappears with large T .\n3. “AR(1) rejected means the model is wrong.” In differences it is expected. Look at AR(2).\n4. “More instruments are always better.” Valid in principle; see Chapter 25.\n\n23.7 What it means for the thesis\nMy inference. If your bank panel includes lagged capital adequacy (CAR) or lagged NPAs, then with T ≈ 15 the within estimator is biased by roughly −0.1 to −0.2 on the persistence parameter; so FE is a lower bound and pooled OLS an upper bound, and a credible GMM estimate should lie between them. If it does not, suspect the instruments. Do you need the original? For the derivations and the logic: no. Read Wooldridge Section 11.6 if you want his sequential-exogeneity framing and the instrument matrix with his notation. Read Arellano–Bond (1991) if you will cite the AR(2) test formula or implement the estimator by hand; it is not in the folder.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ23.1 Which sign has the OLS bias and which the FE bias for ρ?"}, {"pdf": 198, "printed": 171, "ch": 23, "text": "Q23.2 State the Arellano–Bond moment conditions for period t.\nExplain\nQ23.3 Why does FE give a biased estimate even though it removes ci ?\nQ23.4 Why is AR(1) in differences expected but AR(2) a problem?\nApply\nQ23.5 Evaluate −(1 + ρ)/(T − 1) for ρ = 0.7, T = 10.\nQ23.6 How many Arellano–Bond moments for T = 8? For T = 20?\nQ23.7 Why is yi,t−1 not a valid instrument for ∆yi,t−1 ?\nViva\nQ23.8 “With N = 12 I can still use all lags, since the theory allows it.” Respond.\nQ23.9 Argue for or against FE as the main specification in a bank panel with T = 15.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA23.1 OLS upward, FE downward.\nA23.2 E[yis ∆uit ] = 0 for s = 0, . . . , t − 2 (or s = 1, . . . , t − 2 if y0 is unavailable).\nA23.3 Demeaning puts ūi into the transformed error and ui,s for s < t into the transformed regressor; they correlate.\nA23.4 ∆uit and ∆ui,t−1 share ui,t−1 ; AR(2) would mean uit correlates with ui,t−2 , invalidating level lags.\nA23.5 −1.7/9 ≈ −0.189.\nA23.6 7 · 6/2 = 21; 19 · 18/2 = 171.\nA23.7 It contains ui,t−1 , which is also in ∆uit .\nA23.8 Theory allows it asymptotically; with N = 12 the weighting matrix cannot be estimated, the J test has no power and the estimate drifts (Table 22.1; Chapter 25).\nA23.9 For: simple, no instruments; against: bias of order 1/T , still −0.1 to −0.2 here; it can be reported as a lower bound."}, {"pdf": 199, "printed": 172, "ch": 24, "text": "CHAPTER 24\n\nBlundell and Bond (1998): Initial Conditions and Moment Restrictions in Dynamic Panel Data Models Source line Blundell, R. and Bond, S. (1998), Journal of Econometrics 87, 115–143. I read the whole paper from a text extraction of the PDF in the folder. Printed page numbers below are from its running heads. Some formulas were garbled in extraction; where I rely on my own derivation instead of the printed formula I say so and mark it “check against the original”.\n\n24.1 What the paper is about, in three sentences\nDifference GMM (Chapter 23) uses lagged levels as instruments for differences. When the series is persistent, or when bank-specific effects are large relative to the shocks, those instruments are nearly uncorrelated with the differences (“weak instruments”), so the estimator is biased and imprecise in short panels (pp. 121–123, 133–135). Blundell and Bond add moment conditions for the levels equations that use lagged differences as instruments, valid under a mild restriction on the initial condition (pp. 124–126); this is system GMM. The gain is large for persistent series and short T , and the paper illustrates it on a UK firm employment equation (pp. 136– 138).\n\n24.2 The model (pp. 116–118)\nyit = αyi,t−1 + ηi + vit ,\n\nE(ηi ) = E(vit ) = E(ηi vit ) = E(vit vis ) = 0 (t 6= s),\n\n(24.1)\n\nwith E(yi1 vit ) = 0 for t ≥ 2. Define the levels error uit = ηi + vit (the paper’s notation).\n\n24.3 Why difference GMM is weak (Section 3.2, pp. 121–123):\nderivation\nTake T = 3. Then (23.2) has one equation, one instrument yi1 , and the estimator is simple IV, with first stage ∆yi2 = πyi1 + ri ,\n\nfrom the model, ∆yi2 = (α − 1)yi1 + ηi + vi2 .\n\nDerivation of π (mine; the paper gives the result). In the stationary case, Var(yi1 ) = ση2 /(1−α)2 +σv2 /(1−α2 ) and Cov(yi1 , ∆yi2 ) = (α−1)Var(yi1 )+Cov(yi1 , ηi ), with Cov(yi1 , ηi ) ="}, {"pdf": 200, "printed": 173, "ch": 24, "text": "ση2 /(1 − α). Simplifying, Cov(yi1 , ∆yi2 ) = −σv2 /(1 + α) and\n\n−σv2 /(1 + α)\n= (α − 1) 2 2\nση /(1 − α) + σv /(1 − α )\nση /σv + k\n\n(1 − α)2\n1−α\n1−α\n1+α\n\n(24.2)\n\nThis agrees with the paper’s statement that the least-squares estimate of (α − 1) is biased towards zero because E(yi1 ηi ) > 0 (p. 121). Reading: as α → 1, k → 0 so π → 0; as ση2 /σv2 grows, π → 0. First-stage R2 (mine). With s = ση2 /σv2 , Var(∆yi2 ) = 2σv2 /(1 + α), so R2 =\n\nCov2\n(1 − α)2\nVar(yi1 )Var(∆yi2 )\n2(1 + α)(s + k)\n\nIt falls to zero as α → 1 (left panel of Figure 24.1). The paper’s own measure is the concentration parameter q (equation 3.7, p. 121), which it plots against α in its Figure 3; it shows that q → 0 as α → 1 and as ση2 grows, and that the asymptotic standard error of α̂DIF explodes as q → 0 (p. 122–123). I do not reproduce its printed formula, because my text extraction garbled it — check against the original if you want to quote it. Asymptotic variance (mine). For just-identified IV, Avar(α̂) = Var(yi1 ∆ui3 )/[N Cov2 ]. Because yi1 is independent of vi2 , vi3 : Var(yi1 ∆vi3 ) = 2σv2 Var(yi1 ), so N Avar(α̂DIF ) =\n\n2σv2 · σv2 (s + k)/(1 − α)2\n2(1 + α)2 (s + k)\nσv4 /(1 + α)2\n(1 − α)2\n\nFirst-stage R² of Δy₂ on y₁\n\n0.40\n\nση²/σv² = 0.25\nση²/σv² = 1\n\n0.35\n\nση²/σv² = 4\n\n0.30\n0.25\n0.20\n0.15\n0.10\n0.05\n0.00\n0.0\n\n0.2\n\n0.4\n0.6\nPersistence α\n\n0.8\n\n1.0\n\nVar(differences IV) ÷ Var(levels IV), T = 3\n\nIt explodes as α → 1. For the levels IV (next section), using ∆yi2 as instrument for yi2 in the 2 is uncorrelated with u2 (true under levels equation gives, under an approximation that ∆yi2 normality), N Avar = 2(1 + α)(1 + s), which stays finite as α → 1. The ratio is plotted in the right panel of Figure 24.1.\n\n103\n\n102\n\n101\n\n100\n0.0\n\n0.2\n\n0.4\n0.6\nPersistence α\n\n0.8\n\n1.0\n\nFigure 24.1. Left: first-stage R2 of ∆yi2 on yi1 (T = 3), my derivation, for three variance ratios s = ση2 /σv2 . Right: ratio of the asymptotic variance of the difference IV to that of the levels IV (my derivation under the normality approximation). Variance ratios of 100 or more arise for α ≥ 0.9.\n\nI checked these formulas by simulation (500 replications at N large): the simulated interquartilerange scale of the just-identified IV matched the formulas to within about 10%. (The IV estimator in the just-identified case has no finite moments, so I compared interquartile ranges, not variances.)"}, {"pdf": 201, "printed": 174, "ch": 24, "text": "24.4 The extra moment conditions (Section 4, pp. 124–126)\nFor t = 3, . . . , T , the paper adds\nE uit ∆yi,t−1 = 0\n\nwhere uit = ηi + vit ,\n\n(4.3),\n\n(24.3)\n\nwhich are T − 2 linear moments for the levels equation (instruments: lagged differences). The initial-condition requirement (4.4) is E(ui3 ∆yi2 ) = 0. Why they are valid (my derivation of the paper’s argument). Write yi1 = ηi /(1 − α) + ϵi1 (the paper’s ui1 in (4.5) is my ϵi1 ; the deviation of the initial value from its long-run level). Then from the model ∆yi2 = (α − 1)ϵi1 + vi2 . Hence E(ηi ∆yi2 ) = (α − 1)E(ηi ϵi1 ), which is zero if the deviation of the initial condition from the long-run level is uncorrelated with the long-run level (the paper’s condition (4.6): E(ϵi1 ηi ) = 0 and E(ϵi1 vit ) = 0). By footnote 8, for t ≥ 3, ∆yit = αt−2 ∆yi2 +\n\nt−3\n\nαs ∆vi,t−s ,\n\ns=0\n\nand the ∆v terms are uncorrelated with ηi ; so E(ηi ∆yi,t−1 ) = 0 for all later t as well, and (24.3) follows since ∆yi,t−1 is also uncorrelated with vit . What the condition means in economic words\n\nBanks may enter the sample away from their long-run level (the paper says any “entryperiod disequilibrium” from ηi /(1 − α) that is randomly distributed across units preserves the condition, p. 125), but the size of that gap must not be related to the long-run level itself. It fails if, for example, all units start at the same value yi1 = µ, or if the first observation is a draw independent of ηi with the long-run mean still varying (p. 125). For a bank panel, ask: is the first year in the sample special (a regulatory shock, a recapitalisation, a data starting point after a merger)? The paper also shows that, combined with the difference moments, (4.3) and (4.4) imply the non-linear Ahn–Schmidt moments (4.1) and make them redundant (p. 124); so the full set of second-order restrictions can be estimated by linear GMM. Instrument count. System GMM adds T − 2 moments (Figure 23.2), one for each levels equation, using the single instrument ∆yi,t−1 . (As far as I know, further lagged differences are redundant given the difference-equation moments, a result usually credited to Arellano and Bover (1995); I did not verify this in a source.)\n\n24.5 Simulation evidence\nThe paper’s results. Its Monte Carlo (pp. 129–135) finds that at α = 0.8 and N = 200, T = 4 the RMSE of system GMM is about 20% of that of difference GMM in the design with"}, {"pdf": 202, "printed": 175, "ch": 24, "text": "strong permanent effects (“Model A”), and about 50% in the other (“Model B”) (pp. 133–134). The variance ratio in Table 3 for T = 4, Model A, N = 200 is 2.32 at α = 0.3, 3.79 at α = 0.5,\n19.07 at α = 0.8 and 42.55 at α = 0.9 (p. 135).\n\nFE mean\n\nDIF mean (s.d.)\n\nSYS mean (s.d.)\n\n0.5 0.874\n\n−0.034\n\n0.493 (0.187)\n\n0.506 (0.111)\n\n0.8 0.980\n\n0.139\n\n0.665 (0.596)\n\n0.806 (0.125)\n\n0.9 0.995\n\n0.196\n\n0.405 (0.781)\n\n0.922 (0.119)\n\nOLS mean\n\nTable 24.1. My Monte Carlo, N = 200, T = 4, one-step estimators. The OLS–FE bracket is wide; difference GMM is nearly unbiased at α = 0.5 but poor at 0.8 and 0.9; system GMM is close to the truth throughout.\n\n1.4\n\nN = 200, T = 4\n\nN = 12, T = 15\n1.2\n\nMean estimate ± s.d.\n\n1.2\n1.0\n\n1.0\n\n0.8\n\n0.8\n\n0.6\n\n0.6\n\n0.4\n\n0.4\n\n0.2\n0.2\n\n0.0\nOLS\n\n−0.2\nα=0.5\n\nα=0.8\n\nDIF\nSYS\n\nDIF all\nSYS all\n\n0.0\n\nα=0.9\n\nα=0.5\n\nSYS 2 lags\nSYS coll.\n\nα=0.8\n\nFigure 24.2. Left: Table 24.1. Right: a thesis-like design, N = 12 banks, T = 15. Dashed lines mark the true α. With few units, system GMM with every lag (about 104 instruments) is worse than FE at α = 0.5 (mean 0.238, RMSE 0.291), but restricting to two lags (about 38 instruments) gives 0.501, and a collapsed instrument set (3 instruments) gives 0.499. At α = 0.8 the two-lag version has mean 0.875 (s.d. 0.077) and the collapsed one 0.771 (s.d. 0.197). See Chapter 25.\n\nMy own replication (500 replications, ση2 = σv2 ).\n\n24.6 The empirical application (Section 7, pp. 136–138)\nA UK panel of 140 listed manufacturing companies with 7–9 annual observations, 1976–84 (the Arellano–Bond data), and a dynamic employment equation (p. 136). Table 4 (p. 137) shows the key result: The paper reads this as the weak-instrument problem: with a short panel and persistent series, difference GMM is biased towards zero. (Capital elasticity: 0.2 in the short-panel difference estimate, 0.6 with system GMM, p. 137.) Notice the instrument counts: the Sargan test has 25, 37, 79 and 100 degrees of freedom, with 140 firms."}, {"pdf": 203, "printed": 176, "ch": 24, "text": "1979–84 DIF\n\n1979–84 SYS\n\n1976–84 DIF\n\n1976–84 SYS\n\nCoef. on ni,t−1 0.4046 (0.1363)\n\n0.8596 (0.0680)\n\n0.7075 (0.0842)\n\n0.8103 (0.0578)\n\nSargan (df)\n\n43.43 (37)\n\n88.80 (79)\n\n116.05 (100)\n\n15.34 (25)\n\nTable 24.2. Excerpt from Blundell–Bond Table 4 (p. 137). With the short panel, difference GMM gives an implausibly low coefficient on lagged employment; system GMM gives 0.86. With the longer panel the two agree more closely.\n\n24.7 Common misreadings\nMisreadings\n\n1. “System GMM is always better than difference GMM.” It is better when the extra moments are valid and the series persistent; it is not robust to a violation of the initial-condition restriction.\n2. “System GMM assumes stationarity.” It needs the weaker condition (4.6); stationarity is sufficient but not necessary (p. 125).\n3. “The levels instruments are lagged levels.” They are lagged differences, used for the levels equation.\n4. “The J test checks the initial condition.” Only through the difference-in-Hansen test (Chapter 25).\n\n24.8 What it means for the thesis\nMy inference. Bank CAR or NPA series are persistent (autoregressive coefficients of 0.7–0.9 would not be surprising), so difference GMM is the estimator most exposed to weak instruments, and system GMM the natural fix. But the validity of (4.4) deserves an explicit sentence in the thesis. And, as Figure 24.2 shows, with 12 banks the number of instruments matters at least as much as the choice between the two estimators. Do you need the original? For the logic, derivations and use of system GMM: no. Read the original for: the concentration parameter (3.7, p. 121; formula garbled in my extraction), the efficiency comparison in Table 1 (Section 4.4, with the homoskedasticity restrictions), and the conditional GLS estimator (Section 5, not covered here).\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ24.1 Write the model and name the T − 2 extra moment conditions.\nQ24.2 State the condition (4.6) in words.\nExplain"}, {"pdf": 204, "printed": 177, "ch": 24, "text": "Q24.3 Why does the first-stage R2 of differenced IV tend to zero as α → 1?\nQ24.4 Why can lagged differences instrument levels even though lagged levels cannot instrument differences?\nQ24.5 Why does the paper call (4.1) “redundant” once (4.3) and (4.4) are imposed? Apply\nQ24.6 Compute k and π in (24.2) for α = 0.8 and ση2 = σv2 .\nQ24.7 Compute the first-stage R2 for the same values. Compare with α = 0.5.\nQ24.8 Using Table 24.2, by how much does the lagged-dependent coefficient change from DIF to SYS in the short panel? What does the direction suggest? Viva\nQ24.9 A bank panel begins in the year of a major recapitalisation programme. Does that threaten system GMM? Why?\nQ24.10 “If DIF and SYS agree, I am safe; if they disagree, SYS is right.” Discuss.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA24.1 yit = αyi,t−1 + ηi + vit ; E[(ηi + vit )∆yi,t−1 ] = 0, t = 3, . . . , T .\nA24.2 The initial deviation from the long-run level must be uncorrelated with the long-run level.\nA24.3 The series is close to a random walk, so levels carry little information about next period’s change; also the fixed effect is in yi1 but not in the change.\nA24.4 The difference removes ηi , so it is uncorrelated with the levels error component.\nA24.5 Together they imply E(uit ∆yi,t−1 ) = 0 for all t ≥ 3 and the non-linear conditions follow from them.\nA24.6 k = 0.2/1.8 = 0.111; π = −0.2 · 0.111/(1 + 0.111) = −0.020.\nA24.7 R2 = 0.04/(2 · 1.8 · 1.111) = 0.010; at α = 0.5: k = 1/3, R2 = 0.25/(2 · 1.5 · 1.333) = 0.0625.\nA24.8 From 0.40 to 0.86, a rise of 0.45; difference GMM is biased towards zero in a short, persistent panel.\nA24.9 Yes: an initial value away from the long-run level in a way related to the bank’s own level violates (4.6).\nA24.10 Neither: agreement is no guarantee; disagreement may mean either weak instruments in DIF or invalid initial condition for SYS; use the difference-in-Hansen test and the OLS–FE bracket."}, {"pdf": 205, "printed": 178, "ch": 25, "text": "CHAPTER 25\n\nRoodman (2009): Too Many Instruments and How to Do GMM in Practice\nSource line — read this first\nRoodman is not in the folder, and I could not fetch it. This chapter is written from my general knowledge of two papers, which are easily confused:\n• Roodman, D. (2009a), “A note on the theme of too many instruments”, Oxford\nBulletin of Economics and Statistics 71(1), 135–158;\n• Roodman, D. (2009b), “How to do xtabond2: An introduction to difference and\nsystem GMM in Stata”, The Stata Journal 9(1), 86–136.\nPage numbers of arguments inside the papers are not given, and I mark claims I am unsure of “from memory”. Every numerical result in this chapter is my own Monte Carlo (the same design as Chapter 24), not Roodman’s. Before you cite either paper for a specific claim, check it in the original. The supporting methodological papers I mention (Windmeijer 2005; Arellano and Bover 1995) are also not in the folder.\n\n25.1 The central warning\nBoth Arellano–Bond and Blundell–Bond by default use every valid lag as an instrument. The instrument count then grows quadratically in T (Figure 23.2). The warning, as I know the paper’s argument:\n1. Overfitting of the endogenous regressor. With many instruments relative to N , the first stage fits the endogenous variable nearly perfectly, so the second stage reproduces the biased OLS-type correlation. The GMM estimate drifts toward the biased estimator it was supposed to replace (from memory: Roodman describes this as the instruments “overfitting endogenous variables”).\n2. A poorly estimated weighting matrix. The optimal weighting matrix has L(L + 1)/2 distinct elements; with L large relative to N it is estimated with enormous noise (Chapter 21, Table 21.1). Two-step standard errors are then biased downward.\n3. A weakened specification test. The Hansen J test loses its power; p-values drift toward 1. Roodman’s practical advice (from memory) is that a p-value of exactly or nearly 1.000 is itself a warning sign, not a reassurance, and that values in a moderate range are preferable."}, {"pdf": 206, "printed": 179, "ch": 25, "text": "25.2 My simulations: all three symptoms\nSymptom 1: bias grows with the instrument count. Design: system GMM, N = 12\nbanks, T = 15, true α = 0.5, 150 replications per point, limiting the number of lags per period from 1 up to all. Instruments\n\n104\n\nMean α̂ (true 0.5)\n\n0.580 0.503 0.446 0.359 0.303 0.243\n\nTable 25.1. My Monte Carlo (system GMM, N = 12, T = 15). The estimate is close to the truth near 38 instruments and falls steadily away from the truth as the count rises toward 104, falling even below the downward-biased FE value (0.377 here).\n\nMean of system GMM estimate\nTrue α = 0.5\n\n0.55\n\nEstimate\n\n0.50\n0.45\n0.40\n0.35\n0.30\n0.25\n\nNumber of instruments (limit on lags)\n\n100\n\nFigure 25.1. Table 25.1 as a picture. The mean of the system GMM estimate against the number of instruments. More instruments is not more information: with 12 units the extra instruments mainly add overfitting. (At 26 instruments the estimate is biased upwards, 0.58; I did not investigate why.)\n\nSymptom 2: the Hansen test. Table 22.1 (Chapter 22) shows that with 103 degrees of freedom and N = 12 the Hansen p-value is 1.00 in 100% of replications even when the model is correct. The model with 37 degrees of freedom does the same. Only the collapsed design (2 degrees of freedom) shows an approximately uniform p-value. In such a panel a Hansen p-value of 0.99 therefore carries essentially no information. Symptom 3: the combination matters. In the N = 12, T = 15, α = 0.5 design: all lags (104 instruments) gives mean 0.238 and RMSE 0.291; two lags (38 instruments) gives mean 0.501; collapsed (3 instruments) gives 0.499. At α = 0.8 with N = 12: FE 0.634, DIF all lags 0.58, SYS all lags 0.718, SYS two lags 0.875 (s.d. 0.077), SYS collapsed 0.771 (s.d. 0.197). The collapsed version is less biased but noisier.\n\n25.3 The remedies\nCollapsing, explicitly. In the standard difference-GMM matrix, row t uses yi1 , . . . , yi,t−2 in its own block. A collapsed matrix has L = T − 2 columns for yi,t−2 , yi,t−3 , …, one column"}, {"pdf": 207, "printed": 180, "ch": 25, "text": "Remedy\n\nWhat it does\n\nCost\n\nLimit the lags (e.g. lags 2– Fewer columns of Zi ; count linear in Throws away valid 3 only) weaker if ρ high\n\ninformation;\n\nCollapse the instrument One column per lag distance rather Imposes that the moment holds on avmatrix than per (period, lag) pair; count in- erage across periods; less efficient dependent of T Rule of thumb: ments ≤ N\n\ninstru- Keeps Λ̂ sensible (from memory: With 12 banks this is a severe limit\nRoodman cites this as a rough guide,\nnot a theorem)\n\nWindmeijer\n(2005) Corrects the downward bias of two- Does not fix bias or the J test\nfinite-sample correction\nstep standard errors\nForward orthogonal de- Subtract the mean of future obser- Needs complete forward information viations (Arellano–Bover vations instead of the previous one; 1995) keeps more observations when gaps exist and keeps the transformed errors uncorrelated Compare OLS, FE and The GMM estimate of ρ should lie — (a sanity check, my addition from GMM between FE (below) and OLS (above) Chapter 23) Table 25.2. Remedies for instrument proliferation. The descriptions are from my general knowledge of Roodman’s two papers and Windmeijer (2005); I could not check them against the originals.\n\nper distance, stacked over t: all instruments of the same lag distance share a single moment condition. The sample moment then averages across periods, which is why the count no longer depends on T in the way it did.\n\n25.4 Reading a dynamic-panel results table\nThe recommended reporting (from Roodman’s Stata paper, from memory) is: the coefficient table; number of instruments; number of groups; the AR(1) and AR(2) tests in differences; the Hansen J statistic with its degrees of freedom and p-value; and, for system GMM, the difference-in-Hansen test for the level moment conditions (Chapter 21, distance statistic). Use the checklist below.\n\n25.5 Common misreadings\nMisreadings\n\n1. “A Hansen p-value of 0.99 is the best result.” With many instruments it is expected even when the model is wrong.\n2. “Collapsing is a trick to pass the test.” It is a legitimate restriction; but report results both ways.\n3. “Windmeijer fixes small-sample problems.” It fixes the standard errors of the twostep estimator, not the bias."}, {"pdf": 208, "printed": 181, "ch": 25, "text": "Item\n\nHealthy\n\nRed flag\n\nInstruments vs groups\n\nL ≤ N or a clear collapsed/lag-limited L \u001d N with all lags\ndesign\n\nAR(1) in differences\n\nRejected (expected)\n\nNot rejected: check the data\n\nAR(2) in differences\n\nNot rejected\n\nRejected: lags ≥ 2 invalid; use deeper\nlags or revise\n\nHansen p-value\n\nModerate; not rejected\n\n< 0.05; or ≈ 1.00 with many instruments (Table 22.1)\n\nDifference-in-Hansen\n(levels moments)\n\nNot rejected\n\nRejected: initial condition suspect\n\nρ̂ vs OLS and FE\n\nBetween them\n\nOutside the bracket\n\nRobustness\n\nResults stable when instruments are Results change with the instrument\ncollapsed or lags limited\ncount (Figure 25.1)\n\nTable 25.3. Checklist for dynamic panel output.\n\n4. “Roodman 2009 is one paper.” There are two (the OBES note and the Stata Journal guide).\n\n25.6 What it means for the thesis\nMy inference. With 12 banks, the usual instrument-count rule (≤ N ) forces a collapsed or heavily lag-limited design. A defensible workflow: (i) report OLS and FE as bounds; (ii) estimate system GMM with collapsed instruments and with two lags; (iii) report the number of instruments, AR(2), Hansen and the difference-in-Hansen; (iv) show that ρ̂ is stable across designs; (v) state that Hansen p-values are weak evidence at this N . If results are not stable, say so and rely on the bounds. Do you need the original? For the reasoning and for practice: not for the logic, because my simulations show each symptom directly. But for anything you quote or cite from Roodman, yes: his specific recommendations, thresholds and Stata syntax are written here from memory. Read the Stata Journal paper (2009b) before you run xtabond2 and the OBES note (2009a) before you cite the argument.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ25.1 Name three symptoms of instrument proliferation.\nQ25.2 What does collapsing do to the instrument matrix?\nExplain"}, {"pdf": 209, "printed": 182, "ch": 25, "text": "Q25.3 Why does a J test with 100 degrees of freedom and 12 units have almost no power?\nQ25.4 Why do many instruments push the estimate towards the biased estimator?\nQ25.5 Why is the Windmeijer correction not a remedy for bias?\nApply\nQ25.6 With T = 15, give the instrument counts for difference GMM (all lags) and with two lags. (Use Figure 23.2’s formulas.)\nQ25.7 A paper reports N = 14, 52 instruments, Hansen p = 0.98, AR(2) p = 0.60, ρ̂ = 0.31 with OLS 0.92 and FE 0.28. Evaluate. Viva\nQ25.8 Defend a collapsed instrument set in a 12-bank panel to a sceptical examiner.\nQ25.9 “The GMM estimate must lie between OLS and FE.” Is this a theorem?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA25.1 Bias towards the OLS-type estimate, a test that never rejects (p near 1), standard errors too small.\nA25.2 One column per lag distance instead of one per (period, lag) pair; the count no longer grows with T .\nA25.3 The weighting matrix is estimated from 12 observations; the sample moments can be matched almost exactly, so J is small.\nA25.4 The first stage nearly reproduces the regressor itself, so the second stage behaves like OLS-type regression on it.\nA25.5 It adjusts the variance for estimation error in Λ̂ only.\nA25.6 14 · 13/2 = 91. With two lags the formula in Figure 23.2 gives 2(T − 3) + 1 = 25.\nA25.7 52 instruments for 14 groups is far above the rule of thumb; Hansen 0.98 is not reassuring; ρ̂ = 0.31 is close to the FE value 0.28 which is biased downward: the estimate may have drifted. Ask for collapsed estimates.\nA25.8 The moment conditions are valid for the true ρ; collapsing keeps them valid, uses fewer, less noisy moments, and in my simulation recovers the true value (0.499).\nA25.9 No; it is a heuristic from the signs of OLS and FE biases in simple models (Chapter 23); violated if the model is misspecified, so use it as a diagnostic."}, {"pdf": 210, "printed": 183, "ch": 26, "text": "CHAPTER 26\n\nSynthesis: Choosing an Estimator for a\nSmall Bank Panel\n26.1 The one-page map\nEstimator\n\nFixes\n\nPooled OLS\n\nNothing for\nlagged y\n\nFixed effects\n\nFails when\n\nor Always biased upward for ρ\n\nChapter\n\nT small and ρ high: bias ≈ 23\n−(1 + ρ)/(T − 1)\n\nAnderson–Hsiao Endogeneity of ∆yt−1 ρ near 1 (weak); inefficient\nwith one instrument\n\nDifference\nGMM\n\nUses all lags\n\nPersistent series, large ση2 /σv2 ; 23, 24\nmany instruments\n\nSystem GMM\n\nAdds levels moments\n\nInitial-condition\nrestriction 24\nfails; many instruments\n\nCollapsed / lag- Controls instrument Less efficient\nlimited GMM\ncount\n\nTable 26.1. Six estimators and when they fail.\n\n26.2 The logic that links the chapters\n1. Prerequisites (Ch. 19). Projections, convergence, OLS and FE.\n2. IV (Ch. 20). Replace the unusable moment E(x′ u) = 0 by E(z ′ u) = 0; weak instruments make the inconsistency corr(z, u)/corr(z, x) explode.\n3. System GMM machinery (Ch. 21, 22). Many moments, one parameter vector;\noptimal weight Λ−1 ; J ∼ χ2L−K .\n4. Dynamic panels (Ch. 23). Lagged y breaks strict exogeneity; the instrument matrix is a staircase; moments grow as (T − 1)(T − 2)/2.\n5. Blundell–Bond (Ch. 24). Differences have weak levels instruments when ρ → 1; levels moments with difference instruments repair it under (4.4).\n6. Roodman (Ch. 25). The same machinery misbehaves when L is large relative to N ."}, {"pdf": 211, "printed": 184, "ch": 26, "text": "26.3 A decision path for a 12-bank, about-15-year panel (my inference)\nSuggested workflow — my inference, not a rule from any source\n\n1. Write the model and decide which regressors are exogenous, predetermined (sequentially exogenous) or endogenous. Climate-transition measures may be plausibly predetermined; capital ratios and profitability are typically endogenous.\n2. Estimate pooled OLS and FE; record ρ̂ from each. They give the bracket.\n3. Estimate system GMM with collapsed instruments and with lags 2–3 only. Report instruments, groups, AR(1), AR(2), Hansen p, and difference-in-Hansen.\n4. Check that ρ̂ is within the bracket and stable across designs.\n5. Be explicit that with 12 groups the Hansen test is weak (Table 22.1) and that asymptotic standard errors are optimistic; use Windmeijer-corrected errors, and consider reporting the robustness of the sign and magnitude of the key coefficient rather than only its significance.\n6. State the identifying assumptions in words: no serial correlation in the idiosyncratic error; the initial-condition restriction (4.6) if system GMM is used; validity of the chosen lags as instruments.\n\n26.4 Ninety-minute lecture outline\nMinutes\n\nBlock\n\nContent\n\n0–10\n\nWhy OLS and FE fail\n\nUpward bias (pooled), −(1 + ρ)/(T − 1) (FE); Figure 23.1\n\n10–25\n\nIV logic\n\nE(z ′ u) = 0; weak-instrument inconsistency; Figure 20.1\n\n25–40\n\nGMM\n\nObjective, optimal weight, example of Figure 21.1; J test\n\n40–55\n\nArellano–Bond\n\nStaircase instrument matrix; moment counts; AR(2)\n\n55–70\n\nBlundell–Bond\n\nWhy weak; levels moments; condition (4.6); Table 24.1\n\n70–85\n\nRoodman\n\nOverfitting, Hansen test; Table 25.1, Table 22.1\n\n85–90\n\nClose\n\nChecklist, Table 25.3\n\nTable 26.2. A 90-minute lecture.\n\n26.5 Test\nShort answers (4 marks each).\n1. Derive the sign of the bias of pooled OLS for ρ in (23.1).\n2. Derive the number of Arellano–Bond moments for T = 10 observations.\n3. State and explain the null hypothesis of the J test and its degrees of freedom.\n4. Explain why the asymptotically optimal GMM weighting matrix may be a bad choice in a sample of 12 banks."}, {"pdf": 212, "printed": 185, "ch": 26, "text": "Longer questions (10 marks each).\n5. Prove that the GMM variance with weight W exceeds the one with weight Λ−1 by a p.s.d. matrix.\n6. Derive the first-order Nickell bias of the within estimator.\n7. Derive π and the first-stage R2 for the T = 3 difference IV, and explain what happens as α → 1.\n8. Evaluate a published system GMM table with 12 groups, 90 instruments, Hansen p = 0.99 and AR(2) p = 0.4.\n\nAnswer keys\nA26.1 yi,t−1 contains ci , which is in the error; if Var(c) > 0 the covariance is positive; bias upward.\nA26.2 (T − 1)(T − 2)/2 = 36.\nA26.3 Null: all L moment conditions are valid; χ2L−K .\nA26.4 Λ̂ has L(L + 1)/2 distinct elements; estimated from 12 groups; overfitting and a vacuous\nA26.5 Theorem 21.3.\nA26.6 Section “Nickell bias: derivation” of Chapter 23.\nA26.7 Equation (24.2) and the R2 formula; both tend to zero.\nA26.8 90 instruments \u001d 12; Hansen 0.99 expected under proliferation and uninformative; ask for collapsed estimates, bracket check, difference-in-Hansen.\n\n26.6 What is verified, what is not"}, {"pdf": 213, "printed": 186, "ch": 26, "text": "Source\nWooldridge\nCh. 5, 8\n\nStatus\n(2010) Read\n\nNotes\nEquation numbers from the book; the proofs written out are\nmine\n\nWooldridge Ch. 14\n\nPartly read\n\nPage numbers not recorded; the number of the J-statistic\nequation unmatched\n\nWooldridge Ch. 10–11\n\nUsed for set- Nickell derivation and instrument counts are mine\n\nBlundell–Bond (1998)\n\nRead\n(ex- Concentration-parameter formula garbled; my derived π\ntracted text) agrees with the printed structure; check the printed form\n\nRoodman (2009a, b)\n\nNot read\n\nWritten from memory; all numbers are mine\n\nNickell;\nAnderson– Not read\nHsiao; Arellano–Bond;\nWindmeijer; Arellano–\nBover\n\nDescribed from general knowledge\n\nMonte Carlo\n\n500 replications (150 for the instrument sweep), seed fixed;\ncode in the build folder\n\nMine\n\nTable 26.3. Provenance of Phase 4."}], "cards": [{"id": "19.1", "ch": 19, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the WLLN, the CLT and Slutsky’s theorem in one line each.", "a": "WLLN: sample means converge in probability to expectations. CLT: standardised sums are asymptotically normal. Slutsky: continuous functions of convergent sequences converge to the function of the limit; products of p- and d-convergent sequences converge in distribution."}, {"id": "19.2", "ch": 19, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What are the defining properties of PZ and MZ ?", "a": "Symmetric, idempotent; PZ Z = Z, MZ Z = 0."}, {"id": "19.3", "ch": 19, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Define strict exogeneity of xit in model (19.1).", "a": "E(uit |xi1 , . . . , xiT , ci ) = 0 for all t."}, {"id": "19.4", "ch": 19, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is E(xu) = 0 weaker than E(u|x) = 0? Give a case where the first holds and the second does not.", "a": "E(u|x) = 0 implies zero correlation with every function of x; the converse fails: let u = x2 − 1 with x standard normal, so E(xu) = E(x3 ) − E(x) = 0 but E(u|x) 6= 0."}, {"id": "19.5", "ch": 19, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does fixed T matter for the choice of asymptotic framework?", "a": "With T fixed there is no averaging over time to wash out ūi ; all consistency comes from N."}, {"id": "19.6", "ch": 19, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the within transformation remove ci but not create a new problem when xit is strictly exogenous?", "a": "ci cancels exactly; the transformed error üit is uncorrelated with every ẍis under strict exogeneity, so OLS on the transformed data is consistent."}, {"id": "19.7", "ch": 19, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Show that tr(PZ ) = rank(Z) for Z with L columns, and say what this implies about the number of overidentifying restrictions in a regression of x on Z.", "a": "Trace of IL is L, so rank(PZ ) = L; regressing x on Z uses L parameters; with K endogenous regressors the overidentification degree is L − K."}, {"id": "19.8", "ch": 19, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Let x = z + q and u = q + e as in the example. Find the plim of OLS and of IV using z as instrument.", "a": "OLS: β + 0.5. IV with z: Cov(z, u) = 0 and Cov(z, x) = 1, so IV → β."}, {"id": "19.9", "ch": 19, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Compute β̂F E for N = 2, T = 2 with data (y11 , y12 ) = (1, 3), (x11 , x12 ) = (0, 2), (y21 , y22 ) = (2, 3), (x21 , x22 ) = (1, 2).", "a": "Unit 1: ȳ = 2, x̄ = 1, so ÿ = (−1, 1), ẍ = (−1, 1). Unit 2: ȳ = 2.5, x̄ = 1.5, P P 2 ÿ = (−0.5, 0.5), ẍ = (−0.5, 0.5). ẍÿ = (1 + 1) + (0.25 + 0.25) = 2.5; ẍ = (1 + 1) + (0.25 + 0.25) = 2.5. So β̂F E = 1."}, {"id": "19.10", "ch": 19, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Consistency is a large-sample property, so it is irrelevant to a panel of 12 banks.” Discuss.", "a": "Consistency tells you what the estimator is aiming at; it does not tell you the finitesample error. With N = 12 the asymptotic standard errors can be very misleading (Chapters 24–26); consistency is necessary, not sufficient. P"}, {"id": "19.11", "ch": 19, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“FE and FD give the same estimate when T = 2.” Prove it, and say what changes for T > 2.", "a": "For T = 2: ∆yi2 = yi2 − yi1 and ÿi2 = ∆yi2 /2, ÿi1 = −∆yi2 /2, so t ẍit ÿit = ∆xi ∆yi /2 P 2 and ẍit = ∆x2i /2: the ratio is the FD estimator. For T > 2 they differ: FD uses only adjacent differences and is serially correlated if uit is serially uncorrelated; FE is more efficient under serially uncorrelated u."}, {"id": "20.1", "ch": 20, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the two conditions for a valid instrument and say which can be tested.", "a": "Exogeneity (Cov(z, u) = 0), relevance (partial correlation with x). Relevance is testable."}, {"id": "20.2", "ch": 20, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Write the 2SLS estimator in matrix form.", "a": "β̂ = (X ′ PZ X)−1 X ′ PZ y."}, {"id": "20.3", "ch": 20, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What residuals must be used to estimate σ 2 in 2SLS?", "a": "ûi = yi − xi β̂ with the original xi ."}, {"id": "20.4", "ch": 20, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State Theorem 5.3.", "a": "2SLS is efficient among IV estimators with instruments linear in z, under 2SLS.1– 2SLS.3."}, {"id": "20.5", "ch": 20, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Show that 2SLS with one instrument and one regressor equals the simple IV estimator.", "a": "With L = K, X ′ Z is square and invertible, so (X ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ X)−1 = (Z ′ X)−1 (Z ′ Z)(X ′ Z)−1 ; multiplying by X ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ y leaves (Z ′ X)−1 Z ′ y, the simple IV estimator."}, {"id": "20.6", "ch": 20, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is the first stage required to include all exogenous regressors?", "a": "Otherwise the second stage uses an x̂ built with fewer instruments than the model uses; the omitted exogenous variable stays in the error and is correlated with x̂, causing inconsistency."}, {"id": "20.7", "ch": 20, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Wooldridge say that IV has no finite mean when L = K?", "a": "Wooldridge, citing Kinal (1980): the existence of moments depends on the number of overidentifying restrictions; with L = K there are none, so the estimator has no expected value."}, {"id": "20.8", "ch": 20, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain, using (20.1), when IV is worse than OLS.", "a": "When |Corr(z, u)/Corr(z, x)| > |Corr(x, u)|."}, {"id": "20.9", "ch": 20, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Data: z = (0, 0, 1, 1), x = (1, 2, 4, 5), y = (2, 3, 7, 9). Cov(z, y)/Cov(z, x) and the OLS slope. Compute the IV slope", "a": "z̄ groups: z = 0: x mean 1.5, y mean 2.5; z = 1: x mean 4.5, y mean 8. Wald: P (8 − 2.5)/(4.5 − 1.5) = 5.5/3 = 1.833. OLS: x̄ = 3, ȳ = 5.25; (x − x̄)(y − ȳ) = (−2)(−3.25) + (−1)(−2.25) + (1)(1.75) + (2)(3.75) = 6.5 + 2.25 + 1.75 + 7.5 = 18; P (x − x̄)2 = 10; OLS = 1.8."}, {"id": "20.10", "ch": 20, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A regression of the capital ratio on a climate-exposure index uses the exposure of a bank’s state-owned parent as an instrument. Write down the two conditions and say what evidence would support each.", "a": "Exogeneity: the parent’s exposure affects the capital ratio only through the bank’s own exposure (hard to defend: ownership may affect capital rules directly). Relevance: firststage F . Evidence: institutional detail, placebo tests, comparison of first-stage and reduced form."}, {"id": "20.11", "ch": 20, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Given Corr(z, u) = 0.1 and Corr(z, x) = 0.3, Corr(x, u) = 0.25: which is more inconsistent, IV or OLS?", "a": "IV: 0.1/0.3 = 0.333 (times σu /σx ); OLS: 0.25. IV is more inconsistent."}, {"id": "20.12", "ch": 20, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Instruments cannot be tested, so IV results are never credible.” Discuss.", "a": "Not tested but not unarguable: one can argue from institutions, examine reduced forms, run placebo checks and sensitivity analysis. “Never credible” overstates; “never proved” is right."}, {"id": "20.13", "ch": 20, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“An overidentification test that does not reject proves the instruments are valid.” Re153 spond.", "a": "No: failure to reject has low power, especially with weak or few instruments, and with too many instruments it is vacuous (Chapter 25)."}, {"id": "21.1", "ch": 21, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State SIV.1 and SIV.2.", "a": "E(Zi′ ui ) = 0; rank E(Zi′ Xi ) = K. p"}, {"id": "21.2", "ch": 21, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What is the GMM estimator in closed form for a given Ŵ ? What is the optimal Ŵ ?", "a": "β̂ = (X ′ Z Ŵ Z ′ X)−1 X ′ Z Ŵ Z ′ Y ; Ŵ − → Λ−1 ."}, {"id": "21.3", "ch": 21, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What is the null distribution of J, and its degrees of freedom?", "a": "χ2L−K ."}, {"id": "21.4", "ch": 21, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is the GMM estimator consistent for any p.d. W ?", "a": "Because the sample moments converge to zero at the truth and C ′ W C is invertible for any p.d. W ."}, {"id": "21.5", "ch": 21, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain why Ŵ = (Z ′ Z/N )−1 gives 2SLS.", "a": "Substituting Ŵ = (Z ′ Z/N )−1 in (21.4) gives [X ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ X]−1 X ′ Z(Z ′ Z)−1 Z ′ Y , the 2SLS formula."}, {"id": "21.6", "ch": 21, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the optimal weighting matrix depend on the variance of the moments, not of the errors?", "a": "Because the estimator is built from the moments; the precision of a moment, not of an error, decides its weight."}, {"id": "21.7", "ch": 21, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why can adding moment conditions never reduce asymptotic efficiency (with the optimal weight), yet hurt in small samples?", "a": "With the optimal weight an extra moment can be given weight zero, so asymptotic variance cannot rise; in finite samples Λ̂ must be estimated with more elements and the resulting noise biases the estimate."}, {"id": "21.8", "ch": 21, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Compute the asymptotic variance for W =!I and for W = Λ−1 in the example of 2 1", "a": "W = I: G′ ΛG = (1, 1)Λ(1, 1)′ = 2 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 6, G′ G = 2, so V = 6/4 = 1.5. Optimal: ! 2 −1 , G′ Λ−1 G = 13 (2 − 1 − 1 + 2) = 23 , so V = 1.5. Equal: here the −1 2 moments are exchangeable, so equal weights are already optimal. Λ−1 = 13"}, {"id": "21.9", "ch": 21, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A dynamic panel model has 14 moment conditions and 3 parameters. What are the degrees of freedom of J? If J = 26, what is the decision at 5%?", "a": "14 − 3 = 11; the 5% critical value of χ211 is 19.68; 26 > 19.68, reject."}, {"id": "21.10", "ch": 21, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Suppose L = K. What is J? Why?", "a": "J = 0: the moment equations can be solved exactly, so the minimised objective is zero."}, {"id": "21.11", "ch": 21, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Because two-step GMM is efficient, always use it.” Respond.", "a": "Efficiency is asymptotic; with many moments Λ̂ is poorly estimated, the standard errors from two-step GMM are too small, and bias grows (Chapters 24–25)."}, {"id": "21.12", "ch": 21, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“GMM requires fewer assumptions than maximum likelihood, so it is always preferable.” Discuss.", "a": "GMM needs only moment conditions, so it is robust to distributional misspecification, but it is less efficient when the likelihood is correct and, with many moments, it can behave badly in small samples."}, {"id": "22.1", "ch": 22, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Write the GMM objective for general moments g(w, θ).", "a": "θ̂ = arg min ḡN (θ)′ Ŵ ḡN (θ)."}, {"id": "22.2", "ch": 22, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What is the Jacobian G0 in a linear IV model?", "a": "G0 = −E(Zi′ Xi )."}, {"id": "22.3", "ch": 22, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is the mean-value theorem needed to derive the asymptotic distribution?", "a": "The estimator is defined implicitly by a first-order condition nonlinear in θ̂; the expansion linearises it around θ0 so that the CLT applies to a sum."}, {"id": "22.4", "ch": 22, "lv": "Explain", "q": "What does the vanishing cross term in Lemma 14.1 mean statistically?", "a": "The efficient estimator’s error is uncorrelated with the difference, so any inefficient estimator equals the efficient one plus orthogonal noise."}, {"id": "22.5", "ch": 22, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is the J test uninformative when L is large relative to N ?", "a": "The weighting matrix is poorly estimated and the sample moments can be fitted almost perfectly, so the minimised objective is too small."}, {"id": "22.6", "ch": 22, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Show that for g = Z ′ (y −Xθ) the formula VW in Theorem 22.2 reduces to Theorem 21.2.", "a": "With G0 = −C, VW = (C ′ W C)−1 C ′ W ΛW C(C ′ W C)−1 ; the sign cancels in the sandwich."}, {"id": "22.7", "ch": 22, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A model has 60 moments and 4 parameters. Degrees of freedom of J? How many distinct elements does Λ have?", "a": "60 − 4 = 56; 60 · 61/2 = 1830 distinct elements."}, {"id": "22.8", "ch": 22, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Identification in GMM is just a rank condition.” Discuss for nonlinear models.", "a": "Local identification needs the Jacobian to have full column rank, but global identification may fail through multiple solutions; the rank check cannot detect that."}, {"id": "22.9", "ch": 22, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Why is a high J p-value in a panel of 12 banks not good news?", "a": "With few units and many instruments the test has almost no power; see Table 22.1."}, {"id": "23.1", "ch": 23, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Which sign has the OLS bias and which the FE bias for ρ?", "a": "OLS upward, FE downward."}, {"id": "23.2", "ch": 23, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the Arellano–Bond moment conditions for period t.", "a": "E[yis ∆uit ] = 0 for s = 0, . . . , t − 2 (or s = 1, . . . , t − 2 if y0 is unavailable)."}, {"id": "23.3", "ch": 23, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does FE give a biased estimate even though it removes ci ?", "a": "Demeaning puts ūi into the transformed error and ui,s for s < t into the transformed regressor; they correlate."}, {"id": "23.4", "ch": 23, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is AR(1) in differences expected but AR(2) a problem?", "a": "∆uit and ∆ui,t−1 share ui,t−1 ; AR(2) would mean uit correlates with ui,t−2 , invalidating level lags."}, {"id": "23.5", "ch": 23, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Evaluate −(1 + ρ)/(T − 1) for ρ = 0.7, T = 10.", "a": "−1.7/9 ≈ −0.189."}, {"id": "23.6", "ch": 23, "lv": "Apply", "q": "How many Arellano–Bond moments for T = 8? For T = 20?", "a": "7 · 6/2 = 21; 19 · 18/2 = 171."}, {"id": "23.7", "ch": 23, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Why is yi,t−1 not a valid instrument for ∆yi,t−1 ?", "a": "It contains ui,t−1 , which is also in ∆uit ."}, {"id": "23.8", "ch": 23, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“With N = 12 I can still use all lags, since the theory allows it.” Respond.", "a": "Theory allows it asymptotically; with N = 12 the weighting matrix cannot be estimated, the J test has no power and the estimate drifts (Table 22.1; Chapter 25)."}, {"id": "23.9", "ch": 23, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Argue for or against FE as the main specification in a bank panel with T = 15.", "a": "For: simple, no instruments; against: bias of order 1/T , still −0.1 to −0.2 here; it can be reported as a lower bound."}, {"id": "24.1", "ch": 24, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Write the model and name the T − 2 extra moment conditions.", "a": "yit = αyi,t−1 + ηi + vit ; E[(ηi + vit )∆yi,t−1 ] = 0, t = 3, . . . , T ."}, {"id": "24.2", "ch": 24, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the condition (4.6) in words.", "a": "The initial deviation from the long-run level must be uncorrelated with the long-run level."}, {"id": "24.3", "ch": 24, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the first-stage R2 of differenced IV tend to zero as α → 1?", "a": "The series is close to a random walk, so levels carry little information about next period’s change; also the fixed effect is in yi1 but not in the change."}, {"id": "24.4", "ch": 24, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why can lagged differences instrument levels even though lagged levels cannot instrument differences?", "a": "The difference removes ηi , so it is uncorrelated with the levels error component."}, {"id": "24.5", "ch": 24, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the paper call (4.1) “redundant” once (4.3) and (4.4) are imposed?", "a": "Together they imply E(uit ∆yi,t−1 ) = 0 for all t ≥ 3 and the non-linear conditions follow from them."}, {"id": "24.6", "ch": 24, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Compute k and π in (24.2) for α = 0.8 and ση2 = σv2 .", "a": "k = 0.2/1.8 = 0.111; π = −0.2 · 0.111/(1 + 0.111) = −0.020."}, {"id": "24.7", "ch": 24, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Compute the first-stage R2 for the same values. Compare with α = 0.5.", "a": "R2 = 0.04/(2 · 1.8 · 1.111) = 0.010; at α = 0.5: k = 1/3, R2 = 0.25/(2 · 1.5 · 1.333) = 0.0625."}, {"id": "24.8", "ch": 24, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Using Table 24.2, by how much does the lagged-dependent coefficient change from DIF to SYS in the short panel? What does the direction suggest?", "a": "From 0.40 to 0.86, a rise of 0.45; difference GMM is biased towards zero in a short, persistent panel."}, {"id": "24.9", "ch": 24, "lv": "Viva", "q": "A bank panel begins in the year of a major recapitalisation programme. Does that threaten system GMM? Why?", "a": "Yes: an initial value away from the long-run level in a way related to the bank’s own level violates (4.6)."}, {"id": "24.10", "ch": 24, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“If DIF and SYS agree, I am safe; if they disagree, SYS is right.” Discuss.", "a": "Neither: agreement is no guarantee; disagreement may mean either weak instruments in DIF or invalid initial condition for SYS; use the difference-in-Hansen test and the OLS–FE bracket."}, {"id": "25.1", "ch": 25, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Name three symptoms of instrument proliferation.", "a": "Bias towards the OLS-type estimate, a test that never rejects (p near 1), standard errors too small."}, {"id": "25.2", "ch": 25, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does collapsing do to the instrument matrix?", "a": "One column per lag distance instead of one per (period, lag) pair; the count no longer grows with T ."}, {"id": "25.3", "ch": 25, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does a J test with 100 degrees of freedom and 12 units have almost no power?", "a": "The weighting matrix is estimated from 12 observations; the sample moments can be matched almost exactly, so J is small."}, {"id": "25.4", "ch": 25, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why do many instruments push the estimate towards the biased estimator?", "a": "The first stage nearly reproduces the regressor itself, so the second stage behaves like OLS-type regression on it."}, {"id": "25.5", "ch": 25, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is the Windmeijer correction not a remedy for bias?", "a": "It adjusts the variance for estimation error in Λ̂ only."}, {"id": "25.6", "ch": 25, "lv": "Apply", "q": "With T = 15, give the instrument counts for difference GMM (all lags) and with two lags. (Use Figure 23.2’s formulas.)", "a": "14 · 13/2 = 91. With two lags the formula in Figure 23.2 gives 2(T − 3) + 1 = 25."}, {"id": "25.7", "ch": 25, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A paper reports N = 14, 52 instruments, Hansen p = 0.98, AR(2) p = 0.60, ρ̂ = 0.31 with OLS 0.92 and FE 0.28. Evaluate.", "a": "52 instruments for 14 groups is far above the rule of thumb; Hansen 0.98 is not reassuring; ρ̂ = 0.31 is close to the FE value 0.28 which is biased downward: the estimate may have drifted. Ask for collapsed estimates."}, {"id": "25.8", "ch": 25, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Defend a collapsed instrument set in a 12-bank panel to a sceptical examiner.", "a": "The moment conditions are valid for the true ρ; collapsing keeps them valid, uses fewer, less noisy moments, and in my simulation recovers the true value (0.499)."}, {"id": "25.9", "ch": 25, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“The GMM estimate must lie between OLS and FE.” Is this a theorem?", "a": "No; it is a heuristic from the signs of OLS and FE biases in simple models (Chapter 23); violated if the model is misspecified, so use it as a diagnostic."}, {"id": "26.1", "ch": 26, "lv": "Short (4 marks)", "q": "Derive the sign of the bias of pooled OLS for ρ in (23.1).", "a": "yi,t−1 contains ci , which is in the error; if Var(c) > 0 the covariance is positive; bias upward."}, {"id": "26.2", "ch": 26, "lv": "Short (4 marks)", "q": "Derive the number of Arellano–Bond moments for T = 10 observations.", "a": "(T − 1)(T − 2)/2 = 36."}, {"id": "26.3", "ch": 26, "lv": "Short (4 marks)", "q": "State and explain the null hypothesis of the J test and its degrees of freedom.", "a": "Null: all L moment conditions are valid; χ2L−K ."}, {"id": "26.4", "ch": 26, "lv": "Short (4 marks)", "q": "Explain why the asymptotically optimal GMM weighting matrix may be a bad choice in a sample of 12 banks.", "a": "Λ̂ has L(L + 1)/2 distinct elements; estimated from 12 groups; overfitting and a vacuous J."}, {"id": "26.5", "ch": 26, "lv": "Long (10 marks)", "q": "Prove that the GMM variance with weight W exceeds the one with weight Λ−1 by a p.s.d. matrix.", "a": "Theorem 21.3."}, {"id": "26.6", "ch": 26, "lv": "Long (10 marks)", "q": "Derive the first-order Nickell bias of the within estimator.", "a": "Section “Nickell bias: derivation” of Chapter 23."}, {"id": "26.7", "ch": 26, "lv": "Long (10 marks)", "q": "Derive π and the first-stage R2 for the T = 3 difference IV, and explain what happens as α → 1.", "a": "Equation (24.2) and the R2 formula; both tend to zero."}, {"id": "26.8", "ch": 26, "lv": "Long (10 marks)", "q": "Evaluate a published system GMM table with 12 groups, 90 instruments, Hansen p = 0.99 and AR(2) p = 0.4.", "a": "90 instruments ≪ 12; Hansen 0.99 expected under proliferation and uninformative; ask for collapsed estimates, bracket check, difference-in-Hansen."}]};